PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L06U3.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":6,"cefr":"B1b","unit":3,"theme":"Mysteries and Puzzles","essential_question":"How do we use clues to solve problems?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["mystery","clue","detective","suspect","witness","alibi","proof","examine","riddle","logical","deduce","missing","footprint","reveal"],"recycled_words":["clue","evidence","infer","character","setting","problem","solution","theme","suddenly","past simple","stage direction","claim","assume"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS1","title":"Word Power: Mysteries and Puzzles","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about mysteries and puzzles and 4 Tier 2 words (conclude, assume, indicate, investigate)","use five kinds of context clues – definition, synonym, antonym, example and general sense – to infer the meaning of unknown words","find the signal words that point to each kind of context clue"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن الألغاز والأحاجي و4 كلمات أكاديمية.","استخدام خمسة أنواع من قرائن السياق لاستنتاج معاني الكلمات المجهولة.","إيجاد الكلمات الدالة على كل نوع من قرائن السياق."],"success_criteria":["I can use the mystery words correctly in sentences.","I can name the kind of context clue in a sentence and its signal word.","I can infer the meaning of a new word and check it in the sentence."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات الألغاز في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع تسمية نوع قرينة السياق في الجملة وكلمتها الدالة.","أستطيع استنتاج معنى كلمة جديدة والتحقق منه في الجملة."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"mystery","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmɪstəri/","definition":"something strange or unknown that is hard to explain; a story about solving a puzzle or a crime","arabic":"لغز / قصة غموض","example":"The missing book was a mystery.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"clue","pos":"noun","ipa":"/klu/","definition":"a piece of information that helps you solve a problem","arabic":"دليل / مفتاح لغز","example":"A wet footprint was the first clue.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"detective","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈtɛktɪv/","definition":"a person who looks for clues to find out the truth","arabic":"محقّق","example":"The detective examined the room.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"suspect","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsʌˌspɛkt/","definition":"a person who may have done something wrong","arabic":"مشتبه به","example":"There were three suspects.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"witness","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈwɪtnəs/","definition":"a person who sees something happen; to see something happen","arabic":"شاهد / يشهد","example":"The witness saw a man near the door.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"alibi","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæləˌbaɪ/","definition":"proof that a person was somewhere else when something happened","arabic":"حجّة غياب","example":"His alibi was that he was in Room 12.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"proof","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pruf/","definition":"facts or evidence that show something is true","arabic":"برهان / إثبات","example":"The photos were proof that he was innocent.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"examine","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪɡˈzæmɪn/","definition":"to look at something very carefully","arabic":"يفحص","example":"She examined the lock.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"riddle","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɪdəl/","definition":"a question or puzzle with a clever answer","arabic":"أحجية / لغز كلامي","example":"Can you solve this riddle?","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"logical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈlɑdʒɪkəl/","definition":"using clear, sensible reasoning","arabic":"منطقي","example":"That is a logical answer.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"deduce","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈdus/","definition":"to reach an answer by thinking about the facts you have","arabic":"يستنتج","example":"She deduced that someone had a key.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"missing","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈmɪsɪŋ/","definition":"not in the place where it should be; lost","arabic":"مفقود","example":"The missing manuscript was found in the safe.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"footprint","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfʊtˌprɪnt/","definition":"a mark made by a foot or a shoe","arabic":"أثر قدم","example":"There were muddy footprints on the floor.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"reveal","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈvil/","definition":"to show something that was hidden or secret","arabic":"يكشف","example":"The last photo revealed the truth.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"conclude","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kənˈklud/","definition":"to decide something after thinking about all the facts","arabic":"يستنتج / يخلص إلى","example":"We concluded that the book was safe.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"assume","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əˈsum/","definition":"to think something is true without proof","arabic":"يفترض","example":"Don't assume he is guilty.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"indicate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈɪndɪˌkeɪt/","definition":"to show or point to something","arabic":"يشير إلى / يدل على","example":"The wet carpet indicated a leak.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"investigate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪnˈvɛstəˌɡeɪt/","definition":"to try to find out the facts about something","arabic":"يحقّق في","example":"The students investigated the missing book.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"When you meet an **unknown word**, the **words around it** often give **clues** to its meaning. Good readers act like **detectives**: they look for **context clues**, make a smart guess and **check** it in the sentence.\n\nFive kinds of context clues: (1) **Definition** – the meaning is given (*is, means, is called, that is*); (2) **Synonym** – a word with a similar meaning (*or, also, in other words*); (3) **Antonym / contrast** – an opposite meaning (*but, however, unlike, instead of*); (4) **Example** – examples show the meaning (*such as, for example, including*); (5) **General sense** – the whole situation helps you **infer** the meaning (*because, so, after*).\n\nSteps: (1) **stop** at the unknown word; (2) **look** before and after it for signal words; (3) **name** the kind of clue; (4) **infer** a meaning; (5) **replace** the word with your meaning – does the sentence still make sense?","examples":["Definition: *He had an **alibi**, **that is**, proof that he was somewhere else.* → alibi = proof you were somewhere else.","Synonym: *The detective **scrutinized**, **or** examined closely, every corner of the room.* → scrutinize = examine closely.","Antonym: *Omar looked **guilty**, **but** he was actually **innocent**.* → innocent = not guilty.","Example: *She checked the **evidence**, **such as** the photos, the wet carpet and the lock.* → evidence = things that show what happened.","General sense: *The case was empty, so Reem **gasped** in shock.* → gasp = breathe in quickly from surprise.","Definition with *is called*: *A false clue that leads detectives the wrong way **is called** a **red herring**.*","Contrast with *unlike*: ***Unlike** the **careless** caretaker in the rumor, Mr. Salem was very careful.* → careless = not careful.","Example with *including*: *The **suspects**, **including** Omar and Mr. Salem, were questioned.* → suspects = people who might have done it.","General sense: *After hours of searching, the exhausted detectives finally **solved** the case and went home happy.* → solve = find the answer."],"common_errors":["✗ Skipping every unknown word. → ✓ Stop and look for context clues. (Be a word detective.)","✗ Guessing from the first letter only. → ✓ Read the words before AND after. (Use the whole context.)","✗ Missing the contrast word *but*. → ✓ *but* means the new word is the opposite. (Watch the signal words.)","✗ Not checking the guess. → ✓ Replace the word with your meaning and reread. (Check it.)","✗ The detective examine the room. → ✓ The detective examined the room. (Past simple in stories.)","✗ He is suspect. → ✓ He is a suspect. / We suspect him. (suspect (n) needs *a*; suspect (v) = /səˈspɛkt/.)","✗ Assuming means knowing for sure. → ✓ Assume = think it is true WITHOUT proof. (No proof.)"],"arabic":"عندما تقابل كلمة مجهولة، فإن الكلمات المحيطة بها تعطي غالبًا قرائن على معناها، والقارئ الجيد يتصرف كالمحقق: يبحث عن القرائن، ويخمّن تخمينًا ذكيًا، ثم يتحقق منه.\n\nأنواع القرائن: التعريف (is, means, is called)، والمرادف (or, also)، والتضاد (but, however, unlike)، والمثال (such as, for example, including)، والمعنى العام للموقف (because, so).\n\nالخطوات: توقّف عند الكلمة، وابحث قبلها وبعدها عن الكلمات الدالة، وسمِّ نوع القرينة، واستنتج المعنى، ثم ضع المعنى مكان الكلمة وتحقق.\n\nانتبه: suspect اسمًا (مشتبه به) تُنطق بنبر المقطع الأول، وفعلًا (يشتبه) بنبر المقطع الثاني."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"A glass case… empty. A wet carpet… a camera… and three suspects. Welcome to the world of mysteries!","on_screen":"Mysteries and Puzzles 🔍","visual":"A dim school hall with an empty glass case under a spotlight."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Mystery, clue, detective, suspect, witness, alibi, proof, examine, riddle, logical, deduce, missing, footprint, reveal!","on_screen":"mystery · clue · detective · suspect · witness · alibi · proof · examine · riddle · logical · deduce · missing · footprint · reveal","visual":"Each word appears on a case-file card."},{"time":"01:30–02:05","narration":"Investigate. Don't assume. Find what the clues indicate – then conclude!","on_screen":"investigate · assume · indicate · conclude","visual":"A detective crosses out 'assume' and circles 'evidence'."},{"time":"02:05–03:30","narration":"Unknown word? Look for context clues: definition, synonym, antonym, example and general sense!","on_screen":"Definition · Synonym · Antonym · Example · General sense","visual":"Signal words light up around a mystery word."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Stop, look, name, infer – and check. Case closed!","on_screen":"Stop → Look → Name → Infer → Check","visual":"A 'CASE CLOSED' stamp lands on a file."}],"captions_ar":["خزانة زجاجية… فارغة. سجادة مبللة… كاميرا… وثلاثة مشتبه بهم. مرحبًا بك في عالم الألغاز!","لغز، دليل، محقق، مشتبه به، شاهد، حجة غياب، برهان، يفحص، أحجية، منطقي، يستنتج، مفقود، أثر قدم، يكشف!","حقّق ولا تفترض، وجِد ما تدل عليه القرائن، ثم استنتج!","كلمة مجهولة؟ ابحث عن قرائن السياق: التعريف والمرادف والتضاد والمثال والمعنى العام!","توقّف، ابحث، سمِّ، استنتج، ثم تحقّق. أُغلقت القضية!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, mysterious but friendly male voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct mystery word.","instructions_ar":"اختر كلمة اللغز الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"The ___ looked for clues with a magnifying glass and a notebook.","answer":"detective","explanation":"Detective.","explanation_ar":"المحقق.","options":["witness","riddle","detective","footprint"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A man who saw the boat leave the harbor at night is a ___.","answer":"witness","explanation":"Witness.","explanation_ar":"شاهد.","options":["suspect","witness","mystery","proof"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"He couldn't have taken it – he was at his cousin's wedding all evening. That is his ___.","answer":"alibi","explanation":"Alibi.","explanation_ar":"حجة الغياب.","options":["riddle","footprint","clue","alibi"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"*What has hands but cannot clap?* (A clock!) This question is a ___.","answer":"riddle","explanation":"Riddle.","explanation_ar":"أحجية.","options":["riddle","witness","suspect","proof"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"The muddy ___ led from the garden door to the kitchen.","answer":"footprints","explanation":"Footprint.","explanation_ar":"آثار الأقدام.","options":["footprints","alibis","riddles","suspects"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"At the end of the story, the detective will ___ who took the keys.","answer":"reveal","explanation":"Reveal.","explanation_ar":"يكشف.","options":["assume","examine","witness","reveal"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Context clues. Choose.","instructions_ar":"قرائن السياق: اختر.","items":[{"q":"*A red herring, that is, a false clue that leads detectives the wrong way, fooled everyone.* What kind of context clue is this?","answer":"definition","explanation":"*that is* = definition.","explanation_ar":"تعريف.","options":["antonym","example","definition","synonym"],"dok":1,"skill":"context clues","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*The room was **pristine**, not messy at all.* What does *pristine* mean?","answer":"perfectly clean","explanation":"Contrast clue: not messy.","explanation_ar":"نظيف تمامًا.","options":["very dirty","very dark","perfectly clean","very old"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*The detective found several **artifacts** of the crime, such as a glove, a ticket and a broken key.* What kind of clue helps you?","answer":"example","explanation":"*such as* = example.","explanation_ar":"مثال.","options":["example","definition","antonym","synonym"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*Sami was **apprehensive**, or nervous, about entering the dark library.* What does *apprehensive* mean?","answer":"nervous","explanation":"*or* = synonym.","explanation_ar":"متوتر.","options":["happy","sleepy","hungry","nervous"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*The lock was not broken, so Reem **inferred** that someone had used a key.* What does *inferred* mean?","answer":"decided from the evidence","explanation":"General sense.","explanation_ar":"استنتجت.","options":["forgot","decided from the evidence","broke","hid"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*Unlike his **meticulous** sister, who checks every detail, Faris is often careless.* What does *meticulous* mean?","answer":"very careful about details","explanation":"Contrast + definition.","explanation_ar":"دقيق جدًا.","options":["careless","very careful about details","very loud","lazy"],"dok":3,"skill":"context clues","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank. Each word fits one sentence.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"The police will ________ the missing car report tomorrow.","answer":"investigate","explanation":"Find out the facts.","explanation_ar":"يحققون في.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Don't ________ that Omar took it just because he was in the room.","answer":"assume","explanation":"Think without proof.","explanation_ar":"تفترض.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The wet carpet seemed to ________ that there was a leak.","answer":"indicate","explanation":"Show / point to.","explanation_ar":"يدل على.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"After looking at all the photos, Reem could ________ what had happened.","answer":"conclude","explanation":"Decide after thinking.","explanation_ar":"تستنتج.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The time stamps on the photos were ________ that Omar was innocent.","answer":"proof","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"برهان.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Using the clues, the detective was able to ________ where the key was hidden.","answer":"deduce","explanation":"Work out from facts.","explanation_ar":"يستنتج.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words.","instructions_ar":"اكتب باستخدام الكلمات الجديدة.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with a DEFINITION clue for the word *alibi*.","answer":"His alibi, that is, proof that he was somewhere else, was a photo of him at the football match.","explanation":"Definition clue.","explanation_ar":"قرينة تعريف.","dok":2,"skill":"context clues","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a sentence with a CONTRAST clue for the word *logical*.","answer":"Her answer was logical, but his was silly and made no sense.","explanation":"Contrast clue.","explanation_ar":"قرينة تضاد.","dok":2,"skill":"context clues","stage":"production"},{"q":"What is the difference between a *suspect* and a *witness*?","answer":"A suspect is someone who may have done something wrong; a witness is someone who saw it happen.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"production"},{"q":"Why should a detective never *assume*? Give an example.","answer":"Because assuming without proof can blame the wrong person – for example, thinking a boy took a book just because he was nearby.","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"استدلال.","dok":3,"skill":"reasoning","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Detectives","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Case files: the child taps the clue words in a sentence with a magnifying glass, chooses the clue type and the meaning of the unknown word. Each solved case adds a badge to the detective's file.","Teams open a case file and read the sentence.","They circle the signal word and stamp the clue type.","They write their inferred meaning and test it in the sentence.","The teacher reveals the dictionary meaning.","Correct clue type = 1 point; good meaning = 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *a piece of information that helps you solve a problem*?","answer":"clue","explanation":"Clue.","explanation_ar":"دليل.","options":["riddle","clue","alibi","witness"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What are *context clues*?","answer":"words around an unknown word that help you understand it","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"قرائن السياق.","options":["words around an unknown word that help you understand it","pictures on the cover","the last page of a book","a list of answers"],"dok":1,"skill":"context clues"},{"q":"*The detective **examined** the lock with a magnifying glass.* *Examined* means ___.","answer":"looked at very carefully","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"فحص بعناية.","options":["looked at very carefully","broke","painted","ignored"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which signal word usually shows an EXAMPLE clue?","answer":"such as","explanation":"Signal.","explanation_ar":"such as.","options":["but","that is","or","such as"],"dok":1,"skill":"context clues"},{"q":"Which sentence about the detective is correct?","answer":"The detective examined every clue.","explanation":"Past simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The detective examine every clue yesterday.","The detective examining every clue yesterday.","The detective examined every clue.","The detective were examining every clue."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"*The first suspect was **talkative**, but the second hardly said a word.* What does *talkative* mean?","answer":"liking to talk a lot","explanation":"Contrast clue.","explanation_ar":"كثير الكلام.","options":["very quiet","very tall","liking to talk a lot","very sad"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues"},{"q":"*She had no proof, so she shouldn't **assume** he was guilty.* What does *assume* mean here?","answer":"think something is true without proof","explanation":"General sense.","explanation_ar":"تفترض.","options":["know something for sure","forget something","write something down","think something is true without proof"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What is the BEST way to check your guess about a word's meaning?","answer":"Replace the word with your meaning and see if the sentence makes sense.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"استبدل الكلمة بالمعنى.","options":["Replace the word with your meaning and see if the sentence makes sense.","Count its letters.","Ask what color it is.","Skip the sentence."],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues"},{"q":"*The **cryptic** note was so hard to understand that nobody could solve it for days.* What does *cryptic* probably mean?","answer":"mysterious and difficult to understand","explanation":"General sense.","explanation_ar":"غامض.","options":["short and simple","very funny","written in red ink","mysterious and difficult to understand"],"dok":3,"skill":"context clues"},{"q":"Why are good readers like detectives?","answer":"They use clues around a word to work out its meaning and check their answer.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"يستخدمون القرائن ويتحققون.","options":["They wear special hats.","They use clues around a word to work out its meaning and check their answer.","They skip difficult words.","They only read mysteries."],"dok":3,"skill":"word strategies"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'mystery': a question mark over a dark doorway"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'clue': a magnifying glass over a footprint"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'detective': a girl with a notebook and a magnifying glass"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'suspect': three silhouettes with question marks"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'witness': a boy pointing at something he saw"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'alibi': a man fixing an AC in another room, with a clock"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'proof': a photo with a time stamp"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'examine': a hand holding a magnifying glass over a lock"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'riddle': a scroll with a question mark"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'logical': a brain with gears turning"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'deduce': a girl connecting clues with lines"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'missing': an empty glass case"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'footprint': shoe prints on a tiled floor"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'reveal': a curtain opening to show an object"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'conclude': a judge's hammer and a notebook"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'assume': a person jumping to a conclusion across a gap"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'indicate': an arrow pointing to a wet patch"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'investigate': students searching a room with notebooks"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-01","prompt":"A young detective examines an empty display case."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-02","prompt":"Icons for mystery words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS1-03","prompt":"A detective board of context clues."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V01","script":"mystery. The missing book was a mystery."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V02","script":"clue. A wet footprint was the first clue."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V03","script":"detective. The detective examined the room."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V04","script":"suspect. There were three suspects."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V05","script":"witness. The witness saw a man near the door."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V06","script":"alibi. His alibi was that he was in Room 12."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V07","script":"proof. The photos were proof that he was innocent."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V08","script":"examine. She examined the lock."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V09","script":"riddle. Can you solve this riddle?"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V10","script":"logical. That is a logical answer."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V11","script":"deduce. She deduced that someone had a key."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V12","script":"missing. The missing manuscript was found in the safe."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V13","script":"footprint. There were muddy footprints on the floor."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V14","script":"reveal. The last photo revealed the truth."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V15","script":"conclude. We concluded that the book was safe."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V16","script":"assume. Don't assume he is guilty."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V17","script":"indicate. The wet carpet indicated a leak."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-V18","script":"investigate. The students investigated the missing book."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS1-VID","script":"A glass case… empty. A wet carpet… a camera… and three suspects. Welcome to the world of mysteries! Mystery, clue, detective, suspect, witness, alibi, proof, examine, riddle, logical, deduce, missing, footprint, reveal! Investigate. Don't assume. Find what the clues indicate – then conclude! Unknown word? Look for context clues: definition, synonym, antonym, example and general sense! Stop, look, name, infer – and check. Case closed!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS2","title":"The Case of the Missing Manuscript","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read a mystery story of about 460 words","identify the elements of a mystery: the problem, the detective, suspects, clues, a red herring and the solution","explain how the detective uses clues and logical reasoning to solve the case"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة قصة غموض من نحو 460 كلمة.","تحديد عناصر قصة الغموض: المشكلة والمحقق والمشتبه بهم والقرائن والدليل المضلل والحل.","شرح كيف يستخدم المحقق القرائن والاستدلال المنطقي لحل القضية."],"success_criteria":["I can list the suspects and the clues in the story.","I can explain why Omar is a red herring.","I can explain Reem's reasoning step by step with evidence."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع ذكر المشتبه بهم والقرائن في القصة.","أستطيع شرح لماذا كان عمر دليلًا مضللًا.","أستطيع شرح استدلال ريم خطوة بخطوة بالأدلة."],"map_area":"Literary Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"manuscript","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmænjəˌskrɪpt/","definition":"a book or document written by hand","arabic":"مخطوطة","example":"The manuscript was two hundred years old.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"exhibition","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɛksəˈbɪʃən/","definition":"a public show of objects or art","arabic":"معرض","example":"The Heritage Week exhibition opened on Friday.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"gasp","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɡæsp/","definition":"to breathe in quickly because you are surprised or shocked","arabic":"يشهق","example":"Reem gasped when she saw the empty case.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"damp","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/dæmp/","definition":"a little wet","arabic":"رطب / مبتلّ قليلًا","example":"There was a damp patch on the carpet.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"caretaker","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɛrˌteɪkər/","definition":"a person whose job is to look after a building","arabic":"عامل الصيانة / حارس المبنى","example":"The caretaker fixed the air conditioner.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"murmur","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈmɝmər/","definition":"to speak very quietly","arabic":"يتمتم / يهمس","example":"\"Someone had a key,\" she murmured.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"red herring","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌrɛd ˈhɛrɪŋ/","definition":"a false clue that leads people in the wrong direction","arabic":"دليل مضلّل","example":"Omar's camera was a red herring.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **mystery story** has special elements: a **problem** (something missing or a crime), a **detective** who investigates, **suspects** (people who might have done it), **clues** (information that points to the truth), often a **red herring** (a false clue that points the wrong way) and a **solution** that is **revealed** at the end.\n\nThe detective uses **logical reasoning**: she **examines** the evidence, **doesn't assume**, asks questions and connects clues in **order**. Readers can follow the reasoning: *Clue → what it shows → next step*. For example: *The lock was not damaged* → *someone used a key* → *only two people have keys*.\n\nGood mystery writers play **fair**: all the clues are in the story, so a careful reader could solve the case too. When you reread, notice **where** each clue appears and **how** the writer hides it among other details.","examples":["Problem: *The case was empty.* → the manuscript is missing.","Suspects: **Omar** (in the hall alone with a camera) and **Mr. Salem** (has a key).","Clue 1: *The glass was not broken, and the lock was not damaged.* → someone used a key.","Clue 2: *Only two people have keys: me and Mr. Salem.* → Ms. Hana or Mr. Salem opened it.","Clue 3: *a damp patch on the carpet … a brown water stain … across the ceiling* → a leak near the case.","Clue 4 (time order): photos at **1:40** (safe) → **1:55** (water dripping) → **2:10** (empty) → the water came first.","Red herring: *Omar … holding a camera … alone this afternoon!* → it looks suspicious, but his photos prove he is innocent.","Reasoning: water → caretaker with a key → *Upstairs in Room 12 … right above this hall* → Mr. Salem moved it to keep it dry.","Theme: ***Look at the evidence before you accuse anyone.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Thinking the first suspect is always guilty. → ✓ Check every suspect against the evidence. (Don't assume.)","✗ Missing the times on the photos. → ✓ Put events in time order to see what happened first. (Time is a clue.)","✗ Calling Omar's camera a real clue to the thief. → ✓ It is a red herring – it points the wrong way. (False clue.)","✗ The lock was not damage. → ✓ The lock was not damaged. (Passive/adjective → **-ed**.)","✗ Reem deduce that someone had a key. → ✓ Reem deduced that someone had a key. (Past simple → **-d**.)","✗ Saying the solution without evidence. → ✓ Explain each step: clue → what it shows. (Show the reasoning.)","✗ Forgetting the theme. → ✓ The theme: look before you speak. (Mysteries have themes too.)"],"arabic":"لقصة الغموض عناصر خاصة: مشكلة (شيء مفقود أو جريمة)، ومحقق، ومشتبه بهم، وقرائن تشير إلى الحقيقة، وغالبًا دليل مضلّل يشير إلى الاتجاه الخاطئ، وحل يُكشف في النهاية.\n\nيستخدم المحقق الاستدلال المنطقي: يفحص الأدلة، ولا يفترض، ويسأل، ويربط القرائن بالترتيب: قرينة ← ما تدل عليه ← الخطوة التالية.\n\nكاتب الغموض الجيد عادل: كل القرائن موجودة في القصة، فيستطيع القارئ المتأني حل القضية أيضًا.\n\nانتبه: كاميرا عمر دليل مضلّل، والصور بأوقاتها برهان على براءته."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Half past two. A glass case. And… nothing inside.","on_screen":"The Case of the Missing Manuscript 📜🔍","visual":"A spotlight on an empty glass case in a school hall."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"The glass was not broken… the lock was not damaged… and above it, a brown water stain.","on_screen":"Clues 1–3","visual":"Close-ups of the lock, the damp carpet and the ceiling stain."},{"time":"01:30–02:30","narration":"'He was in here alone!' – but the photos tell a different story: 1:40, 1:55, 2:10.","on_screen":"Red herring → proof","visual":"Omar's three photos appear one by one with time stamps."},{"time":"02:30–03:30","narration":"Upstairs in Room 12… a ladder, a towel, and a caretaker who wanted to protect, not to steal.","on_screen":"Solution","visual":"Mr. Salem on a ladder; the manuscript wrapped in cloth in a safe."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Suspects, clues, a red herring and logical reasoning – the recipe for a great mystery!","on_screen":"Problem · Suspects · Clues · Red herring · Solution","visual":"A case file closes with a 'SOLVED' stamp."}],"captions_ar":["الثانية والنصف. خزانة زجاجية. ولا شيء بداخلها.","الزجاج لم يُكسر… والقفل لم يتضرر… وفوقه بقعة ماء بنية.","«كان هنا وحده!» لكن الصور تحكي قصة أخرى: 1:40 و1:55 و2:10.","في الطابق العلوي في الغرفة 12… سلّم ومنشفة وعامل صيانة أراد الحماية لا السرقة.","مشتبه بهم وقرائن ودليل مضلّل واستدلال منطقي: وصفة قصة الغموض الرائعة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, mysterious storyteller female voice, speed 1.0. Separate voices for Reem, Ms. Hana, Omar, Mr. Salem and Grandfather."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Case of the Missing Manuscript","body":"On Thursday afternoon, the school's Heritage Week exhibition was almost ready. In the middle of the hall stood a glass case, and inside it lay the most precious object of all: a two-hundred-year-old handwritten book about navigating by the stars, lent by Reem's grandfather.\n\nAt half past two, Reem came to check on it. She stopped. The case was empty.\n\n\"It's gone!\" she gasped.\n\nWithin minutes, the hall was full of worried people. Ms. Hana, the librarian, turned pale. \"I locked that case myself at one o'clock,\" she said. \"Only two people have keys: me and Mr. Salem, the caretaker.\"\n\nReem took a deep breath. Her grandfather always said, \"A good detective looks before she speaks.\" So she examined the case carefully. The glass was not broken, and the lock was not damaged. \"Whoever opened this had a key,\" she murmured.\n\nThen she noticed something else: a dark, damp patch on the carpet beside the case, and above it, a brown water stain spreading across the ceiling.\n\n\"Look – Omar!\" someone whispered. A Grade 7 boy was standing near the door, holding a camera. \"He was in here alone this afternoon!\"\n\nOmar's face turned red. \"I was taking photos for my history project,\" he said. \"I didn't touch anything!\"\n\nEveryone stared at him. But Reem didn't assume anything. \"Can I see your photos?\" she asked.\n\nOmar nodded and scrolled through his camera. The first photo, taken at 1:40, showed the manuscript safe in its case. The second, taken at 1:55, showed the same case – and a thin line of water dripping from the ceiling onto the carpet. In the third photo, taken at 2:10, the case was already empty.\n\n\"So the water came first,\" Reem said slowly, \"and the manuscript disappeared after that.\" She turned to Ms. Hana. \"Where is Mr. Salem?\"\n\n\"Upstairs in Room 12, fixing a leaking air conditioner,\" the librarian replied. \"Room 12 is right above this hall.\"\n\nReem ran up the stairs with her friend Tariq right behind her. In Room 12, Mr. Salem was standing on a ladder with a towel over his shoulder.\n\n\"Mr. Salem,\" Reem said, \"did you move the manuscript?\"\n\nThe caretaker climbed down, looking confused. Then his eyes widened. \"Oh no! I saw water dripping near the case, so I unlocked it and put the book in the library safe to keep it dry. I meant to leave a note, but they called me up here to stop the leak.\"\n\nTen minutes later, Ms. Hana opened the safe. There, wrapped in a clean cloth, lay the manuscript, perfectly dry.\n\nThat evening, Reem told her grandfather the whole story. He laughed. \"So the thief was really a protector,\" he said. \"And the real detective was the one who looked before she spoke.\"","word_count":462,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U3-LS2-1","genre":"narrative","role":"main","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[{"word":"manuscript","definition":"a book or document written by hand","arabic":"مخطوطة"},{"word":"caretaker","definition":"a person whose job is to look after a building","arabic":"حارس المبنى / عامل الصيانة"},{"word":"safe","definition":"a strong metal box with a lock for keeping valuable things","arabic":"خزنة"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the story. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ القصة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What was the missing manuscript about?","answer":"navigating by the stars","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الملاحة بالنجوم.","options":["the history of Jeddah","navigating by the stars","cooking traditional dishes","Arabic poetry"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Who lent the manuscript to the exhibition?","answer":"Reem's grandfather","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"جد ريم.","options":["Ms. Hana","Mr. Salem","Reem's grandfather","Omar's father"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"When did Ms. Hana lock the case?","answer":"at one o'clock","explanation":"Paragraph 4.","explanation_ar":"الساعة الواحدة.","options":["at one o'clock","at half past two","at 1:55","on Wednesday"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why was Omar in the hall?","answer":"He was taking photos for his history project.","explanation":"Paragraph 8.","explanation_ar":"كان يصوّر لمشروعه.","options":["He was fixing the air conditioner.","He was looking for Reem.","He was taking photos for his history project.","He was cleaning the carpet."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where was Mr. Salem when Reem found him?","answer":"on a ladder in Room 12, upstairs","explanation":"Paragraph 13.","explanation_ar":"على سلّم في الغرفة 12.","options":["in the library","in the hall","at home","on a ladder in Room 12, upstairs"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where was the manuscript at the end?","answer":"in the library safe, wrapped in a clean cloth","explanation":"Paragraph 16.","explanation_ar":"في خزنة المكتبة.","options":["in Omar's camera bag","in the library safe, wrapped in a clean cloth","in Room 12","under the carpet"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Clues, red herrings and reasoning. Choose.","instructions_ar":"القرائن والدليل المضلل والاستدلال: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What did the unbroken glass and lock tell Reem?","answer":"Whoever opened the case had a key.","explanation":"Clue.","explanation_ar":"من فتحها كان معه مفتاح.","options":["Whoever opened the case had a key.","The manuscript was never in the case.","Omar broke the case.","The case was open all day."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why is Omar a red herring in the manuscript mystery?","answer":"He seems suspicious, but the evidence shows he is innocent.","explanation":"Red herring.","explanation_ar":"يبدو مشتبهًا لكنه بريء.","options":["He is the real thief.","He has a key.","He seems suspicious, but the evidence shows he is innocent.","He hid the manuscript in the safe."],"dok":2,"skill":"red herrings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What did Omar's three photos prove?","answer":"The water dripped before the manuscript disappeared.","explanation":"Evidence + time order.","explanation_ar":"الماء سبق الاختفاء.","options":["Omar opened the case.","The water dripped before the manuscript disappeared.","Ms. Hana took the book.","The ceiling was dry."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why was it important that Room 12 is *right above this hall*?","answer":"It explained where the leak came from and why Mr. Salem was there.","explanation":"Clue.","explanation_ar":"يفسر مصدر التسرب.","options":["It showed Omar's classroom.","It was where the safe was.","It was where the exhibition started.","It explained where the leak came from and why Mr. Salem was there."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does *A good detective looks before she speaks* mean in the manuscript story?","answer":"Check the evidence before you accuse anyone.","explanation":"Theme.","explanation_ar":"افحص الأدلة قبل الاتهام.","options":["Detectives should never talk.","Detectives must have good eyes.","Check the evidence before you accuse anyone.","Always speak first, then look."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does Grandfather call the 'thief' *a protector*?","answer":"Mr. Salem moved the manuscript to keep it safe from the water.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"نقلها لحمايتها.","options":["Mr. Salem was a police officer.","Mr. Salem protected Omar.","Mr. Salem stole it to sell it.","Mr. Salem moved the manuscript to keep it safe from the water."],"dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence that shows Reem refuses to jump to a conclusion about Omar.","answer":"But Reem didn't assume anything.","explanation":"Character.","explanation_ar":"الشخصية.","evidence":"But Reem didn't assume anything.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L06-U3-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"character","stage":"freer","sentences":["On Thursday afternoon, the school's Heritage Week exhibition was almost ready.","In the middle of the hall stood a glass case, and inside it lay the most precious object of all: a two-hundred-year-old handwritten book about navigating by the stars, lent by Reem's grandfather.","At half past two, Reem came to check on it.","She stopped.","The case was empty.","\"It's gone!\" she gasped.","Within minutes, the hall was full of worried people.","Ms.","Hana, the librarian, turned pale.","\"I locked that case myself at one o'clock,\" she said.","\"Only two people have keys: me and Mr.","Salem, the caretaker.\"","Reem took a deep breath.","Her grandfather always said, \"A good detective looks before she speaks.\"","So she examined the case carefully.","The glass was not broken, and the lock was not damaged.","\"Whoever opened this had a key,\" she murmured.","Then she noticed something else: a dark, damp patch on the carpet beside the case, and above it, a brown water stain spreading across the ceiling.","\"Look – Omar!\" someone whispered.","A Grade 7 boy was standing near the door, holding a camera.","\"He was in here alone this afternoon!\"","Omar's face turned red.","\"I was taking photos for my history project,\" he said.","\"I didn't touch anything!\"","Everyone stared at him.","But Reem didn't assume anything.","\"Can I see your photos?\" she asked.","Omar nodded and scrolled through his camera.","The first photo, taken at 1:40, showed the manuscript safe in its case.","The second, taken at 1:55, showed the same case – and a thin line of water dripping from the ceiling onto the carpet.","In the third photo, taken at 2:10, the case was already empty.","\"So the water came first,\" Reem said slowly, \"and the manuscript disappeared after that.\"","She turned to Ms.","Hana.","\"Where is Mr.","Salem?\"","\"Upstairs in Room 12, fixing a leaking air conditioner,\" the librarian replied.","\"Room 12 is right above this hall.\"","Reem ran up the stairs with her friend Tariq right behind her.","In Room 12, Mr.","Salem was standing on a ladder with a towel over his shoulder.","\"Mr.","Salem,\" Reem said, \"did you move the manuscript?\"","The caretaker climbed down, looking confused.","Then his eyes widened.","\"Oh no!","I saw water dripping near the case, so I unlocked it and put the book in the library safe to keep it dry.","I meant to leave a note, but they called me up here to stop the leak.\"","Ten minutes later, Ms.","Hana opened the safe.","There, wrapped in a clean cloth, lay the manuscript, perfectly dry.","That evening, Reem told her grandfather the whole story.","He laughed.","\"So the thief was really a protector,\" he said.","\"And the real detective was the one who looked before she spoke.\""]},{"q":"Underline the sentence where Reem states what the photos prove.","answer":"\"So the water came first,\" Reem said slowly, \"and the manuscript disappeared after that.\"","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"الاستدلال.","evidence":"\"So the water came first,\" Reem said slowly, \"and the manuscript disappeared after that.\"","text_id":"TXT-RW-L06-U3-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"freer","sentences":["On Thursday afternoon, the school's Heritage Week exhibition was almost ready.","In the middle of the hall stood a glass case, and inside it lay the most precious object of all: a two-hundred-year-old handwritten book about navigating by the stars, lent by Reem's grandfather.","At half past two, Reem came to check on it.","She stopped.","The case was empty.","\"It's gone!\" she gasped.","Within minutes, the hall was full of worried people.","Ms.","Hana, the librarian, turned pale.","\"I locked that case myself at one o'clock,\" she said.","\"Only two people have keys: me and Mr.","Salem, the caretaker.\"","Reem took a deep breath.","Her grandfather always said, \"A good detective looks before she speaks.\"","So she examined the case carefully.","The glass was not broken, and the lock was not damaged.","\"Whoever opened this had a key,\" she murmured.","Then she noticed something else: a dark, damp patch on the carpet beside the case, and above it, a brown water stain spreading across the ceiling.","\"Look – Omar!\" someone whispered.","A Grade 7 boy was standing near the door, holding a camera.","\"He was in here alone this afternoon!\"","Omar's face turned red.","\"I was taking photos for my history project,\" he said.","\"I didn't touch anything!\"","Everyone stared at him.","But Reem didn't assume anything.","\"Can I see your photos?\" she asked.","Omar nodded and scrolled through his camera.","The first photo, taken at 1:40, showed the manuscript safe in its case.","The second, taken at 1:55, showed the same case – and a thin line of water dripping from the ceiling onto the carpet.","In the third photo, taken at 2:10, the case was already empty.","\"So the water came first,\" Reem said slowly, \"and the manuscript disappeared after that.\"","She turned to Ms.","Hana.","\"Where is Mr.","Salem?\"","\"Upstairs in Room 12, fixing a leaking air conditioner,\" the librarian replied.","\"Room 12 is right above this hall.\"","Reem ran up the stairs with her friend Tariq right behind her.","In Room 12, Mr.","Salem was standing on a ladder with a towel over his shoulder.","\"Mr.","Salem,\" Reem said, \"did you move the manuscript?\"","The caretaker climbed down, looking confused.","Then his eyes widened.","\"Oh no!","I saw water dripping near the case, so I unlocked it and put the book in the library safe to keep it dry.","I meant to leave a note, but they called me up here to stop the leak.\"","Ten minutes later, Ms.","Hana opened the safe.","There, wrapped in a clean cloth, lay the manuscript, perfectly dry.","That evening, Reem told her grandfather the whole story.","He laughed.","\"So the thief was really a protector,\" he said.","\"And the real detective was the one who looked before she spoke.\""]},{"q":"Why does the author include the exact times on Omar's photos?","answer":"so readers can follow the order of events and solve the case too","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"ليتمكن القارئ من حل القضية.","options":["to show that Omar's camera is expensive","so readers can follow the order of events and solve the case too","to make the story longer","to show what time school ends"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Which detail did the author hide early as a clue to the solution?","answer":"a brown water stain spreading across the ceiling","explanation":"Fair clue.","explanation_ar":"بقعة الماء في السقف.","options":["a brown water stain spreading across the ceiling","Reem's grandfather lent the book","Omar's face turned red","Ms. Hana turned pale"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain the mystery.","instructions_ar":"اشرح قصة الغموض.","items":[{"q":"List the suspects and the evidence for and against each one.","answer":"Omar: alone in the hall with a camera (for), but his photos show he didn't touch it (against). Mr. Salem: has a key (for); he moved the book to protect it, not to steal it.","explanation":"Suspects.","explanation_ar":"المشتبه بهم.","dok":2,"skill":"mystery elements","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain Reem's reasoning in four steps.","answer":"1. The lock wasn't broken, so someone used a key. 2. Only Ms. Hana and Mr. Salem had keys. 3. The photos showed water dripping before the book disappeared. 4. Mr. Salem was fixing the leak upstairs, so he probably moved the book to keep it dry.","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"الاستدلال.","dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"production"},{"q":"How might the story have ended if Reem had assumed Omar was guilty?","answer":"Omar would have been blamed unfairly, he would have felt terrible, and the manuscript might have stayed lost in the safe.","explanation":"Consider.","explanation_ar":"تأمّل.","dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"production"},{"q":"What lesson can you take from Reem into your own life?","answer":"I should check the facts before I blame a classmate when something goes missing.","explanation":"Connect.","explanation_ar":"ربط.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"The Case of the Missing Manuscript\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “The Case of the Missing Manuscript”.","answer":"462 words","title":"The Case of the Missing Manuscript","body":"On Thursday afternoon, the school's Heritage Week exhibition was almost ready. In the middle of the hall stood a glass case, and inside it lay the most precious object of all: a two-hundred-year-old handwritten book about navigating by the stars, lent by Reem's grandfather.\n\nAt half past two, Reem came to check on it. She stopped. The case was empty.\n\n\"It's gone!\" she gasped.\n\nWithin minutes, the hall was full of worried people. Ms. Hana, the librarian, turned pale. \"I locked that case myself at one o'clock,\" she said. \"Only two people have keys: me and Mr. Salem, the caretaker.\"\n\nReem took a deep breath. Her grandfather always said, \"A good detective looks before she speaks.\" So she examined the case carefully. The glass was not broken, and the lock was not damaged. \"Whoever opened this had a key,\" she murmured.\n\nThen she noticed something else: a dark, damp patch on the carpet beside the case, and above it, a brown water stain spreading across the ceiling.\n\n\"Look – Omar!\" someone whispered. A Grade 7 boy was standing near the door, holding a camera. \"He was in here alone this afternoon!\"\n\nOmar's face turned red. \"I was taking photos for my history project,\" he said. \"I didn't touch anything!\"\n\nEveryone stared at him. But Reem didn't assume anything. \"Can I see your photos?\" she asked.\n\nOmar nodded and scrolled through his camera. The first photo, taken at 1:40, showed the manuscript safe in its case. The second, taken at 1:55, showed the same case – and a thin line of water dripping from the ceiling onto the carpet. In the third photo, taken at 2:10, the case was already empty.\n\n\"So the water came first,\" Reem said slowly, \"and the manuscript disappeared after that.\" She turned to Ms. Hana. \"Where is Mr. Salem?\"\n\n\"Upstairs in Room 12, fixing a leaking air conditioner,\" the librarian replied. \"Room 12 is right above this hall.\"\n\nReem ran up the stairs with her friend Tariq right behind her. In Room 12, Mr. Salem was standing on a ladder with a towel over his shoulder.\n\n\"Mr. Salem,\" Reem said, \"did you move the manuscript?\"\n\nThe caretaker climbed down, looking confused. Then his eyes widened. \"Oh no! I saw water dripping near the case, so I unlocked it and put the book in the library safe to keep it dry. I meant to leave a note, but they called me up here to stop the leak.\"\n\nTen minutes later, Ms. Hana opened the safe. There, wrapped in a clean cloth, lay the manuscript, perfectly dry.\n\nThat evening, Reem told her grandfather the whole story. He laughed. \"So the thief was really a protector,\" he said. \"And the real detective was the one who looked before she spoke.\"","words":462,"limit":256}],"time_limit_sec":256},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS2-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Detective's Case File","instructions":"Organizer: Fill in each part of the case with evidence from the story.","items":[{"q":"The problem","answer":"The handwritten navigation book is missing from the locked glass case."},{"q":"The suspects","answer":"Omar (alone in the hall with a camera); Mr. Salem (has a key); Ms. Hana (has a key but was the one who locked it)"},{"q":"The clues (at least three)","answer":"unbroken glass and lock; only two keys; damp carpet and ceiling stain; photos at 1:40, 1:55, 2:10"},{"q":"The red herring and why it is false","answer":"Omar's camera – it looks suspicious, but his photos prove the water came first and he didn't touch anything."},{"q":"The detective's reasoning (step by step)","answer":"key used → only two key holders → leak above the case → Room 12 is upstairs → Mr. Salem was fixing it"},{"q":"The solution and the theme","answer":"Mr. Salem moved the book to the safe to keep it dry. Theme: look at the evidence before you accuse anyone."}]}],"game":{"name":"Case Closed!","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Detective board: the child drags clue cards onto the board and connects them with string to the right suspect; red herrings must go in the 'false lead' tray. A correct chain unlocks the safe and reveals the manuscript.","Teams receive clue cards and suspect cards.","They sort the cards into true clues and red herrings.","They connect the true clues in order with string to reach the solution.","They explain the chain aloud: 'This clue shows… so…'.","Correct sorting = 1 point per card; clear chain = 3 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *red herring* in a mystery?","answer":"a false clue that points the wrong way","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"دليل مضلّل.","options":["a false clue that points the wrong way","the real thief","the final solution","the title of the story"],"dok":1,"skill":"mystery elements"},{"q":"What is a *manuscript*?","answer":"a book or document written by hand","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مخطوطة.","options":["a kind of camera","a glass case","a book or document written by hand","a school timetable"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*There was a **damp** patch on the carpet.* *Damp* means ___.","answer":"a little wet","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"رطب.","options":["very hot","a little wet","torn","bright red"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Who had keys to the glass case?","answer":"Ms. Hana and Mr. Salem","explanation":"Key detail.","explanation_ar":"هناء وسالم.","options":["Ms. Hana and Mr. Salem","Omar and Reem","Reem's grandfather only","Tariq and Omar"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details"},{"q":"Which sentence about Reem is correct?","answer":"Reem examined the lock carefully.","explanation":"Past simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Reem examine the lock carefully yesterday.","Reem examining the lock carefully.","Reem were examining the lock carefully.","Reem examined the lock carefully."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which clue showed that the water came BEFORE the manuscript disappeared?","answer":"the times on Omar's photos","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"أوقات الصور.","options":["Ms. Hana's pale face","the towel on Mr. Salem's shoulder","the times on Omar's photos","Tariq running behind Reem"],"dok":2,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"Why did Mr. Salem forget to leave a note?","answer":"He was called upstairs to stop the leak.","explanation":"Cause.","explanation_ar":"استُدعي لإيقاف التسرب.","options":["He was called upstairs to stop the leak.","He wanted to keep the book.","He couldn't write.","Omar told him not to."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect"},{"q":"What kind of person is Reem?","answer":"careful and fair","explanation":"Character traits.","explanation_ar":"دقيقة ومنصفة.","options":["careless and quick to blame","shy and silent","angry and rude","careful and fair"],"dok":2,"skill":"character"},{"q":"How does the mystery writer *play fair* with readers?","answer":"All the clues needed to solve the case appear in the story before the solution.","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"كل القرائن موجودة قبل الحل.","options":["The solution is never revealed.","All the clues needed to solve the case appear in the story before the solution.","The thief is introduced only in the last line.","There are no clues at all."],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"Which statement BEST expresses the theme of *The Case of the Missing Manuscript*?","answer":"Check the evidence before you accuse anyone.","explanation":"Theme.","explanation_ar":"افحص الأدلة قبل الاتهام.","options":["Cameras are dangerous.","Caretakers are forgetful.","Check the evidence before you accuse anyone.","Old books should stay at home."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'manuscript': an old handwritten book with a leather cover"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'exhibition': glass cases in a hall with visitors"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'gasp': a girl with her hand over her mouth"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'damp': a dark wet spot on a carpet"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'caretaker': a man on a ladder with tools"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'murmur': a girl speaking softly to herself"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'red herring': a red fish pointing the wrong way on a sign"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-01","prompt":"Reem examines the empty case and the damp carpet."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-02","prompt":"Three time-stamped photos of the display case."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS2-03","prompt":"A detective's case file organizer."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V01","script":"manuscript. The manuscript was two hundred years old."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V02","script":"exhibition. The Heritage Week exhibition opened on Friday."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V03","script":"gasp. Reem gasped when she saw the empty case."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V04","script":"damp. There was a damp patch on the carpet."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V05","script":"caretaker. The caretaker fixed the air conditioner."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V06","script":"murmur. \"Someone had a key,\" she murmured."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-V07","script":"red herring. Omar's camera was a red herring."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-T1","script":"On Thursday afternoon, the school's Heritage Week exhibition was almost ready. In the middle of the hall stood a glass case, and inside it lay the most precious object of all: a two-hundred-year-old handwritten book about navigating by the stars, lent by Reem's grandfather. At half past two, Reem came to check on it. She stopped. The case was empty. \"It's gone!\" she gasped. Within minutes, the hall was full of worried people. Ms. Hana, the librarian, turned pale. \"I locked that case myself at one o'clock,\" she said. \"Only two people have keys: me and Mr. Salem, the caretaker.\" Reem took a deep breath. Her grandfather always said, \"A good detective looks before she speaks.\" So she examined the case carefully. The glass was not broken, and the lock was not damaged. \"Whoever opened this had a key,\" she murmured. Then she noticed something else: a dark, damp patch on the carpet beside the case, and above it, a brown water stain spreading across the ceiling. \"Look – Omar!\" someone whispered. A Grade 7 boy was standing near the door, holding a camera. \"He was in here alone this afternoon!\" Omar's face turned red. \"I was taking photos for my history project,\" he said. \"I didn't touch anything!\" Everyone stared at him. But Reem didn't assume anything. \"Can I see your photos?\" she asked. Omar nodded and scrolled through his camera. The first photo, taken at 1:40, showed the manuscript safe in its case. The second, taken at 1:55, showed the same case – and a thin line of water dripping from the ceiling onto the carpet. In the third photo, taken at 2:10, the case was already empty. \"So the water came first,\" Reem said slowly, \"and the manuscript disappeared after that.\" She turned to Ms. Hana. \"Where is Mr. Salem?\" \"Upstairs in Room 12, fixing a leaking air conditioner,\" the librarian replied. \"Room 12 is right above this hall.\" Reem ran up the stairs with her friend Tariq right behind her. In Room 12, Mr. Salem was standing on a ladder with a towel over his shoulder. \"Mr. Salem,\" Reem said, \"did you move the manuscript?\" The caretaker climbed down, looking confused. Then his eyes widened. \"Oh no! I saw water dripping near the case, so I unlocked it and put the book in the library safe to keep it dry. I meant to leave a note, but they called me up here to stop the leak.\" Ten minutes later, Ms. Hana opened the safe. There, wrapped in a clean cloth, lay the manuscript, perfectly dry. That evening, Reem told her grandfather the whole story. He laughed. \"So the thief was really a protector,\" he said. \"And the real detective was the one who looked before she spoke.\""},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS2-VID","script":"Half past two. A glass case. And… nothing inside. The glass was not broken… the lock was not damaged… and above it, a brown water stain. 'He was in here alone!' – but the photos tell a different story: 1:40, 1:55, 2:10. Upstairs in Room 12… a ladder, a towel, and a caretaker who wanted to protect, not to steal. Suspects, clues, a red herring and logical reasoning – the recipe for a great mystery!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS3","title":"Inference from clues: \"The Riddle in Room 12\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["make inferences by combining text clues with what I already know","infer characters' feelings and traits from dialogue and stage directions","compare how inference is used to solve problems in the mystery story and the play"],"objectives_ar":["عمل استنتاجات بالجمع بين قرائن النص وما أعرفه مسبقًا.","استنتاج مشاعر الشخصيات وصفاتها من الحوار والإرشادات المسرحية.","مقارنة استخدام الاستنتاج لحل المشكلات في قصة الغموض والمسرحية."],"success_criteria":["I can explain an inference with *The text says… I know… So I infer…*.","I can infer how a character feels from a stage direction and give evidence.","I can compare how Reem and the students in Room 12 use clues."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع شرح استنتاجي بعبارة: النص يقول… وأنا أعرف… لذا أستنتج…","أستطيع استنتاج مشاعر الشخصية من إرشاد مسرحي مع الدليل.","أستطيع مقارنة استخدام ريم وطلاب الغرفة 12 للقرائن."],"map_area":"Literary Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"infer","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪnˈfɝ/","definition":"to decide that something is true from clues and what you know","arabic":"يستنتج","example":"From the clues, I infer that the answer is a clock.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"inference","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪnfərəns/","definition":"an idea you work out from clues and what you know","arabic":"استنتاج","example":"My inference is that Sultan was confused.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"background knowledge","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbækˌɡraʊnd ˈnɑlɪdʒ/","definition":"what you already know from your life and learning","arabic":"المعرفة السابقة","example":"Use your background knowledge about keyboards.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"dial","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdaɪəl/","definition":"a round part you turn to choose a number","arabic":"قرص (الأرقام)","example":"Noura turned the dials of the lock.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"tape","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/teɪp/","definition":"to stick something with sticky plastic strip","arabic":"يلصق / شريط لاصق","example":"A note was taped under the keyboard.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"record","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɛkərd/","definition":"the best result anyone has ever had","arabic":"رقم قياسي","example":"Seven minutes was a new class record.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"grin","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɡrɪn/","definition":"to smile widely","arabic":"يبتسم ابتسامة عريضة","example":"Sultan grinned at his team.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"Writers don't tell readers everything. They leave **clues**, and readers **infer** the rest. An **inference** = **text clues** + **what I know** (background knowledge). Use the frame: ***The text says… I know that… So I infer that…***\n\nIn a **play**, look for clues in **dialogue** and **stage directions**. *(scratching his head)* → I know people scratch their heads when they are puzzled → Sultan is **confused**. *(jumping up)* → excited. *(grinning)* → happy and proud. A good inference is **supported** by evidence – it is not a wild guess.\n\nRiddles are **inference puzzles**: each line is a clue. Combine all the clues, test your answer against **every** line, and check whether it fits. If one clue doesn't fit (a piano has no *space*), think again!","examples":["Riddle clues: *keys … space … enter* + I know a keyboard has a space bar and an Enter key → **a keyboard**.","Riddle clues: *a face and two hands … cannot clap* + I know clocks have a face and hands → **a clock**.","Stage direction: *(scratching his head)* + I know this shows puzzlement → **Sultan is confused**.","Stage direction: *(She looks slowly around the room.)* → **Lina is thinking carefully**.","Stage direction: *(counting on her fingers)* → **Lina is working out the numbers**.","Dialogue: *Next time, can we have twenty riddles?* → **Sultan enjoyed the puzzle**.","Testing a guess: *A piano has keys* – but *a piano doesn't have space* → the guess doesn't fit every clue.","Mystery story: *The glass was not broken* + I know a locked case needs a key → **someone used a key**.","Theme inference: *Every great answer starts with a good question.* → asking questions helps you solve problems."],"common_errors":["✗ Guessing without clues. → ✓ Base your inference on text clues. (Evidence first.)","✗ Using only one clue in a riddle. → ✓ Test your answer against every clue. (All clues must fit.)","✗ Writing what the text already says. → ✓ An inference goes beyond the words. (Read between the lines.)","✗ Sultan is scratching his head because his head itches. → ✓ He is confused – he doesn't understand the riddle. (Think about the situation.)","✗ I infer that Lina feel proud. → ✓ I infer that Lina feels proud. (She → **feels**.)","✗ Forgetting background knowledge. → ✓ Ask: What do I already know about this? (Clues + knowledge.)","✗ Changing your inference without new evidence. → ✓ Change it only when a new clue doesn't fit. (Stay logical.)"],"arabic":"الكتّاب لا يقولون كل شيء، بل يتركون قرائن ليستنتج القارئ الباقي. الاستنتاج = قرائن النص + ما أعرفه مسبقًا. استخدم الإطار: النص يقول… وأنا أعرف أن… لذا أستنتج أن…\n\nفي المسرحية ابحث عن القرائن في الحوار والإرشادات المسرحية؛ فحكّ الرأس يدل على الحيرة، والقفز يدل على الحماس، والابتسامة العريضة تدل على الفرح والفخر.\n\nالأحاجي ألغاز استنتاج: كل سطر قرينة، فاجمع القرائن واختبر إجابتك على كل سطر، وإن لم تنطبق قرينة واحدة ففكّر من جديد.\n\nانتبه: الاستنتاج الجيد مدعوم بالدليل، وليس تخمينًا عشوائيًا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"A locked box. A folded note. Ten minutes. Can you solve the riddle in Room 12?","on_screen":"The Riddle in Room 12 🔐","visual":"A wooden box with a number lock on a teacher's desk."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"I have keys, but I open no locks… What has a space bar and an Enter key?","on_screen":"Riddle 1 → keyboard","visual":"Clue words float up and join into a keyboard."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"A face and two hands, but I cannot clap… Two weeks and a triangle… one, four, three!","on_screen":"Riddle 2 → clock → 1-4-3","visual":"A clock on the wall, a card behind it, dials turning to 1-4-3."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Text clues plus what you know equals an inference!","on_screen":"Clues + What I know = Inference","visual":"An equation builds on a chalkboard."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Scratching his head? Jumping up? Grinning? Stage directions are clues to feelings too!","on_screen":"Stage directions → feelings","visual":"Three students act out the stage directions."}],"captions_ar":["صندوق مقفل. ورقة مطوية. عشر دقائق. هل تستطيع حل لغز الغرفة 12؟","لديّ مفاتيح لكني لا أفتح الأقفال… ما الذي فيه مفتاح مسافة ومفتاح إدخال؟","لي وجه ويدان لكني لا أصفق… أسبوعان ومثلث… واحد، أربعة، ثلاثة!","قرائن النص + ما تعرفه = استنتاج!","يحكّ رأسه؟ يقفز؟ يبتسم ابتسامة عريضة؟ الإرشادات المسرحية قرائن على المشاعر أيضًا!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, playful female voice, speed 1.0. Separate young voices for Noura, Lina, Faris and Sultan; a warm male voice for Mr. Adel."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Riddle in Room 12","body":"**Characters:** Mr. Adel (the teacher), Noura, Lina, Faris, Sultan\n\n**Scene:** Room 12 on Puzzle Day. A small wooden box with a number lock sits on the teacher's desk. A folded note lies beside it.\n\n**MR. ADEL:** *(smiling)* Inside this box is a prize for the team that opens it. Your first clue is in the note. You have ten minutes. *(He leaves the room.)*\n\n**NOURA:** *(unfolding the note and reading aloud)* \"I have keys, but I open no locks. I have space, but I have no room. You can enter, but you can't go inside.\"\n\n**SULTAN:** *(scratching his head)* Keys but no locks? A piano has keys!\n\n**LINA:** But a piano doesn't have space… *(She looks slowly around the room.)* Wait. Space… enter… keys… What has a space bar and an Enter key?\n\n**FARIS:** *(jumping up)* The computer keyboard!\n\n*(They rush to the computer. Faris lifts the keyboard. A second note is taped underneath.)*\n\n**FARIS:** *(reading)* \"I have a face and two hands, but I cannot clap. Look behind me for the numbers.\"\n\n**NOURA:** A face and hands… *(pointing at the wall)* The clock!\n\n*(Sultan climbs carefully onto a chair and looks behind the clock.)*\n\n**SULTAN:** There's a card! It says: \"The code is the number of days in two weeks, followed by the number of sides on a triangle.\"\n\n**LINA:** *(counting on her fingers)* Two weeks is fourteen days. A triangle has three sides. So… one, four, three!\n\n*(Noura turns the dials. Click! The lid opens.)*\n\n**NOURA:** *(lifting out a book)* The Big Book of Riddles! And there's a note: \"Well done, detectives! Every great answer starts with a good question.\"\n\n*(Mr. Adel appears at the door, clapping.)*\n\n**MR. ADEL:** Seven minutes. That's a new class record!\n\n**SULTAN:** *(grinning)* Next time, can we have twenty riddles?","word_count":297,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U3-LS3-1","genre":"drama","role":"paired","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[{"word":"dial","definition":"a round part you turn to choose a number","arabic":"قرص الأرقام"},{"word":"space bar","definition":"the long key on a keyboard that makes a space","arabic":"مفتاح المسافة"},{"word":"record","definition":"the best result anyone has ever had","arabic":"رقم قياسي"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read *The Riddle in Room 12*. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المسرحية واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What was on Mr. Adel's desk at the start of the play?","answer":"a wooden box with a number lock and a folded note","explanation":"Scene.","explanation_ar":"صندوق بقفل أرقام وورقة مطوية.","options":["a computer and a clock","a riddle book","a wooden box with a number lock and a folded note","a trophy"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How long did the team have to open the box?","answer":"ten minutes","explanation":"Mr. Adel's first line.","explanation_ar":"عشر دقائق.","options":["seven minutes","ten minutes","twenty minutes","one hour"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where was the second note?","answer":"taped under the computer keyboard","explanation":"Stage direction.","explanation_ar":"ملصقة تحت لوحة المفاتيح.","options":["behind the clock","inside the box","in Mr. Adel's pocket","taped under the computer keyboard"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What was the code for the lock?","answer":"1-4-3","explanation":"Lina's line.","explanation_ar":"1-4-3.","options":["7-2-1","1-4-3","3-4-1","2-1-4"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What was inside the box?","answer":"The Big Book of Riddles and a note","explanation":"Noura's line.","explanation_ar":"كتاب الأحاجي الكبير وملاحظة.","options":["a new keyboard","a clock","prize money","The Big Book of Riddles and a note"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How long did the team take to open the box?","answer":"seven minutes","explanation":"Mr. Adel's last line.","explanation_ar":"سبع دقائق.","options":["seven minutes","ten minutes","fourteen minutes","three minutes"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Make inferences. Choose.","instructions_ar":"استنتج: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Why did Sultan's guess *a piano* NOT fit the first riddle?","answer":"A piano has keys, but it doesn't have space or an Enter key.","explanation":"Test every clue.","explanation_ar":"لا ينطبق على كل القرائن.","options":["A piano is too big for Room 12.","Pianos have locks.","A piano has keys, but it doesn't have space or an Enter key.","Mr. Adel doesn't like pianos."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What can you infer about Sultan from *(scratching his head)*?","answer":"He is confused by the riddle.","explanation":"Stage direction.","explanation_ar":"محتار.","options":["He is very angry.","He is sleepy.","He is confused by the riddle.","He has solved the riddle."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What can you infer about Faris from *(jumping up)*?","answer":"He is excited because he has found the answer.","explanation":"Stage direction.","explanation_ar":"متحمس.","options":["He is scared of the computer.","He is excited because he has found the answer.","He wants to leave the room.","He hurt his leg."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which background knowledge helped the team solve the code card?","answer":"Two weeks have fourteen days, and a triangle has three sides.","explanation":"Background knowledge.","explanation_ar":"أسبوعان 14 يومًا والمثلث 3 أضلاع.","options":["Clocks have numbers.","Two weeks have fourteen days, and a triangle has three sides.","Keyboards have letters.","Boxes have lids."],"dok":2,"skill":"background knowledge","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What can you infer about Sultan from his last line, *Next time, can we have twenty riddles?*","answer":"He enjoyed solving the riddles.","explanation":"Dialogue.","explanation_ar":"استمتع بالأحاجي.","options":["He thought the riddles were boring.","He wants to stop playing.","He is worried about the time.","He enjoyed solving the riddles."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does the note *Every great answer starts with a good question* suggest about how to solve problems?","answer":"Asking good questions helps you find answers.","explanation":"Theme.","explanation_ar":"الأسئلة الجيدة تقود إلى الإجابات.","options":["Asking good questions helps you find answers.","Questions are not important.","Only teachers ask questions.","Answers come before questions."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Inference Chart.","instructions_ar":"أكمل جدول الاستنتاج.","items":[{"q":"Riddle 1: write the clues, what you know and your inference.","answer":"Clues: keys but no locks, space but no room, enter but can't go inside. I know a keyboard has keys, a space bar and an Enter key. So I infer the answer is a keyboard.","explanation":"Riddle 1.","explanation_ar":"الأحجية 1.","dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Riddle 2 and the code card: write your inferences with the clues.","answer":"A face and two hands but cannot clap → a clock. Two weeks = 14 days; a triangle = 3 sides → code 1-4-3.","explanation":"Riddle 2.","explanation_ar":"الأحجية 2.","dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Choose one stage direction and infer the character's feeling.","answer":"(counting on her fingers) – I know people count on their fingers when they work out numbers – so Lina is concentrating hard.","explanation":"Character.","explanation_ar":"الشخصية.","dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write one inference Reem made in *The Case of the Missing Manuscript* with the clue and her knowledge.","answer":"The text says the lock was not damaged. Reem knows a locked case needs a key. So she infers that someone with a key opened it.","explanation":"Mystery story.","explanation_ar":"قصة الغموض.","dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Compare and create.","instructions_ar":"قارن وأنشئ.","items":[{"q":"How are Reem and the students in Room 12 ALIKE as problem-solvers?","answer":"Both look carefully at clues, test their ideas against the evidence and use what they already know to reach the answer.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"How are the two texts DIFFERENT in form and purpose?","answer":"The mystery story is a narrative that solves a real problem with clues; the play is a drama for actors, and its riddles are a fun puzzle game.","explanation":"Contrast.","explanation_ar":"باين.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write your own three-line riddle about an object in your classroom.","answer":"I have legs but cannot walk. I have a back but cannot bend. You sit on me all day. (A chair.)","explanation":"Create.","explanation_ar":"أنشئ.","dok":2,"skill":"creating riddles","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain the clues in your riddle and how a reader could infer the answer.","answer":"Legs that don't walk and a back that doesn't bend point to furniture; 'you sit on me' shows it is a chair.","explanation":"Explain.","explanation_ar":"اشرح.","dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"The Riddle in Room 12\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “The Riddle in Room 12”.","answer":"297 words","title":"The Riddle in Room 12","body":"**Characters:** Mr. Adel (the teacher), Noura, Lina, Faris, Sultan\n\n**Scene:** Room 12 on Puzzle Day. A small wooden box with a number lock sits on the teacher's desk. A folded note lies beside it.\n\n**MR. ADEL:** *(smiling)* Inside this box is a prize for the team that opens it. Your first clue is in the note. You have ten minutes. *(He leaves the room.)*\n\n**NOURA:** *(unfolding the note and reading aloud)* \"I have keys, but I open no locks. I have space, but I have no room. You can enter, but you can't go inside.\"\n\n**SULTAN:** *(scratching his head)* Keys but no locks? A piano has keys!\n\n**LINA:** But a piano doesn't have space… *(She looks slowly around the room.)* Wait. Space… enter… keys… What has a space bar and an Enter key?\n\n**FARIS:** *(jumping up)* The computer keyboard!\n\n*(They rush to the computer. Faris lifts the keyboard. A second note is taped underneath.)*\n\n**FARIS:** *(reading)* \"I have a face and two hands, but I cannot clap. Look behind me for the numbers.\"\n\n**NOURA:** A face and hands… *(pointing at the wall)* The clock!\n\n*(Sultan climbs carefully onto a chair and looks behind the clock.)*\n\n**SULTAN:** There's a card! It says: \"The code is the number of days in two weeks, followed by the number of sides on a triangle.\"\n\n**LINA:** *(counting on her fingers)* Two weeks is fourteen days. A triangle has three sides. So… one, four, three!\n\n*(Noura turns the dials. Click! The lid opens.)*\n\n**NOURA:** *(lifting out a book)* The Big Book of Riddles! And there's a note: \"Well done, detectives! Every great answer starts with a good question.\"\n\n*(Mr. Adel appears at the door, clapping.)*\n\n**MR. ADEL:** Seven minutes. That's a new class record!\n\n**SULTAN:** *(grinning)* Next time, can we have twenty riddles?","words":297,"limit":169}],"time_limit_sec":169},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Inference Chart","instructions":"Organizer: For each part, write the text clue, what you already know and your inference.","items":[{"q":"Riddle 1: text clues","answer":"keys but no locks; space but no room; enter but can't go inside"},{"q":"Riddle 1: what I know → inference","answer":"A keyboard has keys, a space bar and an Enter key → the keyboard."},{"q":"Riddle 2: text clues → inference","answer":"a face and two hands; cannot clap → the clock"},{"q":"Code card: clues → what I know → inference","answer":"two weeks; sides on a triangle → 14 days and 3 sides → code 1-4-3"},{"q":"A character: stage direction → what I know → inference","answer":"(scratching his head) → people do this when puzzled → Sultan is confused."},{"q":"The mystery story: a clue → what I know → Reem's inference","answer":"The lock was not broken → locked cases need a key → someone with a key opened it."}]}],"game":{"name":"Riddle Lock Race","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Escape-room mode: the child reads a riddle, taps the object in a classroom picture where the next clue is hidden, and finally enters a three-digit code. Each correct inference opens a door.","Each team receives the first riddle.","They solve it and go to the object to find the next riddle.","The last card gives a number puzzle for the lock code.","Teams must explain each inference with 'The clue says… I know… So…'.","First team to open the lock with correct explanations wins 5 points; others get 1 point per riddle solved."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is an *inference*?","answer":"an idea you work out from clues and what you know","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"استنتاج.","options":["a sentence copied from the text","the title of a play","an idea you work out from clues and what you know","a kind of riddle answer key"],"dok":1,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"What is *background knowledge*?","answer":"what you already know from your life and learning","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"المعرفة السابقة.","options":["what you already know from your life and learning","the picture behind a stage","the last page of a book","a list of new words"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Sultan **grinned** at his team.* *Grinned* means ___.","answer":"smiled widely","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"ابتسم ابتسامة عريضة.","options":["smiled widely","shouted angrily","cried quietly","yawned"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which of these is a clue to a character's feeling in a play?","answer":"a stage direction such as *(jumping up)*","explanation":"Stage directions.","explanation_ar":"الإرشاد المسرحي.","options":["the list of characters","the title","the scene number","a stage direction such as *(jumping up)*"],"dok":1,"skill":"stage directions"},{"q":"Which sentence about Lina is correct?","answer":"I infer that Lina feels proud of her answer.","explanation":"She → feels.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["I infer that Lina feels proud of her answer.","I infer that Lina feel proud of her answer.","I infer that Lina feeling proud of her answer.","I infer that Lina are proud of her answer."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Riddle: *I have a neck but no head, and I wear a cap.* What am I?","answer":"a bottle","explanation":"Inference from clues.","explanation_ar":"زجاجة.","options":["a giraffe","a shirt","a pen","a bottle"],"dok":2,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"What can you infer from *(She looks slowly around the room.)*?","answer":"Lina is thinking carefully and searching for an answer.","explanation":"Stage direction.","explanation_ar":"تفكر بعناية.","options":["Lina is bored.","Lina is leaving.","Lina is thinking carefully and searching for an answer.","Lina is cleaning the room."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"Why should you test your answer against EVERY line of a riddle?","answer":"An answer is correct only if it fits all the clues.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"لأن الإجابة يجب أن تطابق كل القرائن.","options":["Riddles have only one line.","The first line is never important.","It makes the riddle longer.","An answer is correct only if it fits all the clues."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"Which inference about Mr. Adel is BEST supported by the play?","answer":"He enjoys challenging his students and is proud of them.","explanation":"Evidence: prize, clapping, 'new class record'.","explanation_ar":"يحب التحدي وفخور بطلابه.","options":["He doesn't like puzzles.","He enjoys challenging his students and is proud of them.","He forgot the code himself.","He wanted the students to fail."],"dok":3,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"How is solving a riddle like solving a mystery?","answer":"In both, you combine clues with what you know and test your answer.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"كلاهما جمع للقرائن مع المعرفة.","options":["In both, you guess without clues.","In both, you combine clues with what you know and test your answer.","Both always need a computer.","Both are only for adults."],"dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'infer': a thinking child with a light bulb and a clue card"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'inference': a puzzle piece fitting into a head"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'background knowledge': a backpack full of books and pictures"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'dial': a number lock with three dials"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tape': a paper note stuck with tape"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'record': a trophy with a stopwatch"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'grin': a boy with a big smile"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-01","prompt":"Students solving a riddle in Room 12."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-02","prompt":"Riddle clues turning into answers."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS3-03","prompt":"Text clue plus what I know equals inference."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V01","script":"infer. From the clues, I infer that the answer is a clock."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V02","script":"inference. My inference is that Sultan was confused."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V03","script":"background knowledge. Use your background knowledge about keyboards."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V04","script":"dial. Noura turned the dials of the lock."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V05","script":"tape. A note was taped under the keyboard."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V06","script":"record. Seven minutes was a new class record."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-V07","script":"grin. Sultan grinned at his team."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-T1","script":"**Characters:** Mr. Adel (the teacher), Noura, Lina, Faris, Sultan **Scene:** Room 12 on Puzzle Day. A small wooden box with a number lock sits on the teacher's desk. A folded note lies beside it. **MR. ADEL:** *(smiling)* Inside this box is a prize for the team that opens it. Your first clue is in the note. You have ten minutes. *(He leaves the room.)* **NOURA:** *(unfolding the note and reading aloud)* \"I have keys, but I open no locks. I have space, but I have no room. You can enter, but you can't go inside.\" **SULTAN:** *(scratching his head)* Keys but no locks? A piano has keys! **LINA:** But a piano doesn't have space… *(She looks slowly around the room.)* Wait. Space… enter… keys… What has a space bar and an Enter key? **FARIS:** *(jumping up)* The computer keyboard! *(They rush to the computer. Faris lifts the keyboard. A second note is taped underneath.)* **FARIS:** *(reading)* \"I have a face and two hands, but I cannot clap. Look behind me for the numbers.\" **NOURA:** A face and hands… *(pointing at the wall)* The clock! *(Sultan climbs carefully onto a chair and looks behind the clock.)* **SULTAN:** There's a card! It says: \"The code is the number of days in two weeks, followed by the number of sides on a triangle.\" **LINA:** *(counting on her fingers)* Two weeks is fourteen days. A triangle has three sides. So… one, four, three! *(Noura turns the dials. Click! The lid opens.)* **NOURA:** *(lifting out a book)* The Big Book of Riddles! And there's a note: \"Well done, detectives! Every great answer starts with a good question.\" *(Mr. Adel appears at the door, clapping.)* **MR. ADEL:** Seven minutes. That's a new class record! **SULTAN:** *(grinning)* Next time, can we have twenty riddles?"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS3-VID","script":"A locked box. A folded note. Ten minutes. Can you solve the riddle in Room 12? I have keys, but I open no locks… What has a space bar and an Enter key? A face and two hands, but I cannot clap… Two weeks and a triangle… one, four, three! Text clues plus what you know equals an inference! Scratching his head? Jumping up? Grinning? Stage directions are clues to feelings too!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Building suspense","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["identify techniques writers use to build suspense","use short sentences, sound words, body reactions, questions and slow-motion details to build suspense","delay the reveal and end a scene with a twist or a cliffhanger"],"objectives_ar":["تحديد الأساليب التي يستخدمها الكتّاب لبناء التشويق.","استخدام الجمل القصيرة وكلمات الأصوات وردود الفعل الجسدية والأسئلة والتفاصيل البطيئة لبناء التشويق.","تأخير الكشف وإنهاء المشهد بمفاجأة أو بنهاية معلّقة."],"success_criteria":["I can name four suspense techniques in a model text and explain their effect.","I can rewrite a flat sentence to make it suspenseful.","I can write a short suspense scene that delays the reveal."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع تسمية أربعة أساليب تشويق في النص النموذجي وشرح أثرها.","أستطيع إعادة كتابة جملة عادية لتصبح مشوّقة.","أستطيع كتابة مشهد تشويق قصير يؤخّر الكشف."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"suspense","pos":"noun","ipa":"/səˈspɛns/","definition":"the excited, worried feeling of waiting to find out what will happen","arabic":"تشويق / ترقّب","example":"The story was full of suspense.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"freeze","pos":"verb","ipa":"/friz/","definition":"to stop moving suddenly because you are afraid (past: froze)","arabic":"يتجمّد في مكانه","example":"He froze when he heard the noise.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"creak","pos":"verb","ipa":"/krik/","definition":"to make a long, high sound, like an old door","arabic":"يصرّ (الباب)","example":"The door creaked open.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"echo","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/ˈɛkoʊ/","definition":"to be heard again because a sound bounces off walls","arabic":"يتردّد صداه / صدى","example":"His laugh echoed down the corridor.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"cliffhanger","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈklɪfˌhæŋər/","definition":"an exciting ending that leaves the reader wondering what happens next","arabic":"نهاية معلّقة مشوّقة","example":"The chapter ended with a cliffhanger.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"twist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/twɪst/","definition":"a surprising change in a story","arabic":"مفاجأة / تحوّل غير متوقع","example":"The kitten was a funny twist.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"onomatopoeia","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɑnəˌmɑtəˈpiə/","definition":"a word that sounds like the noise it describes, such as *buzz* or *bang*","arabic":"محاكاة الصوت","example":"*Scratch* and *creak* are onomatopoeia.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"**Suspense** is the feeling of **waiting nervously** to find out what will happen. Mystery writers build it so readers **can't stop reading**. The secret: make the reader **ask questions** and **delay the answers**.\n\nTechniques: (1) an **unsafe setting** – dark, quiet, alone; (2) **sound words** (*creak, scratch, thud*); (3) **short sentences** for tense moments; (4) **body reactions** instead of feeling words (*his heart pounded* instead of *he was scared*); (5) **questions** in the character's mind (*Who – or what – was inside?*); (6) **slow motion** – describe small actions one by one (*He turned the handle slowly… slowly…*); (7) **a delayed reveal** – show a small part first (*two yellow eyes*), then the whole.\n\nEnd a suspense scene with a **twist** (a surprise) or a **cliffhanger** (stop at the most exciting moment). Use **ellipses (…)** and **dashes (–)** carefully – a few are powerful; too many are tiring.","examples":["Flat: *Khalid heard a noise in the storeroom.* → Suspense: ***As he passed the old storeroom, he heard it. Scratch. Scratch. Scratch.***","Unsafe setting: ***The school was silent. Everyone had gone home.***","Short sentences: ***He froze. The sound stopped.***","Body reactions: ***Khalid's heart pounded. His hand shook as he reached for the handle.***","Question: ***Who – or what – was inside?***","Slow motion: ***He turned it slowly… slowly…***","Delayed reveal: ***In the darkness, two yellow eyes stared back at him.***","Twist: ***A thin gray kitten blinked up at him.***","Cliffhanger (another ending): ***Then the lights went out.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Telling the answer too early: It was a kitten making a noise. → ✓ Delay the reveal until the end. (Keep the secret.)","✗ Long, slow sentences at the scariest moment. → ✓ Use short sentences when the tension is high. (Short = tense.)","✗ He was very very scared. → ✓ His heart pounded, and his hands shook. (Show, don't tell.)","✗ Too many ellipses… everywhere… all the time… → ✓ Use one or two at key moments. (Less is more.)","✗ He froze. The sound stops. → ✓ He froze. The sound stopped. (Keep the past tense.)","✗ The door creak open. → ✓ The door creaked open. (Past simple → **-ed**.)","✗ An ending with no surprise or question. → ✓ Add a twist or a cliffhanger. (End strong.)"],"arabic":"التشويق هو الشعور بالترقّب والقلق لمعرفة ما سيحدث، والسر هو أن تجعل القارئ يطرح أسئلة ثم تؤخّر الإجابات.\n\nأساليب التشويق: مكان موحش (مظلم، هادئ، وحيد)، وكلمات الأصوات، والجمل القصيرة في اللحظات المتوترة، وردود الفعل الجسدية بدل كلمات المشاعر، والأسئلة في ذهن الشخصية، والحركة البطيئة، والكشف المتأخر.\n\nاختم المشهد بمفاجأة أو بنهاية معلّقة، واستخدم علامات الحذف (…) والشرطة (–) باعتدال.\n\nانتبه: حافظ على زمن الماضي في السرد: froze, stopped, creaked."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Why can't you stop reading a good mystery? It's called suspense – and you can learn to build it!","on_screen":"Building Suspense 😱📖","visual":"A reader turns pages faster and faster."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"The school was silent… Scratch. Scratch. Scratch. He froze…","on_screen":"(model text)","visual":"A dark corridor, a storeroom door, a boy's shaking hand on the handle."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"Short sentences. Sound words. A pounding heart. A question. Slow motion…","on_screen":"7 suspense tools","visual":"Tools appear one by one in a detective's bag."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Two yellow eyes… and then – meow! A twist!","on_screen":"Delayed reveal → twist","visual":"The door opens; yellow eyes; a gray kitten."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Or stop at the scariest moment – a cliffhanger! Then the lights went out…","on_screen":"Twist or cliffhanger?","visual":"The screen goes black with a click."}],"captions_ar":["لماذا لا تستطيع التوقف عن قراءة قصة غموض جيدة؟ إنه التشويق، ويمكنك أن تتعلم بناءه!","كانت المدرسة صامتة… خربشة. خربشة. خربشة. تجمّد في مكانه…","جمل قصيرة، كلمات أصوات، قلب يخفق، سؤال، حركة بطيئة…","عينان صفراوان… ثم – مياو! مفاجأة!","أو توقّف عند أكثر اللحظات إثارة: نهاية معلّقة! ثم انطفأت الأنوار…"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, low, suspenseful male voice that becomes cheerful at the twist, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"The Noise in the Storeroom (by Abdullah)","body":"The school was silent. Everyone had gone home, and only Khalid was left, looking for the football he had forgotten. As he passed the old storeroom at the end of the corridor, he heard it. *Scratch. Scratch. Scratch.*\n\nHe froze. The sound stopped. Then it came again – closer this time, right behind the door.\n\nKhalid's heart pounded. His hand shook as he reached for the handle. Who – or what – was inside? The metal was cold under his fingers. He turned it slowly… slowly…\n\nThe door creaked open. In the darkness, two yellow eyes stared back at him.\n\n\"Meow!\"\n\nA thin gray kitten blinked up at him, its paw caught in a roll of tape. Khalid laughed so loudly that the sound echoed down the empty corridor.","word_count":126,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U3-LS4-1","genre":"model text","role":"model","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Setting that feels unsafe:** *The school was silent… only Khalid was left.* – alone, quiet, late.","**Sound words (onomatopoeia):** *Scratch. Scratch. Scratch.* / *creaked* – the reader hears the danger.","**Short sentences:** *He froze. The sound stopped.* – they make the reader read faster and feel tense.","**Body reactions:** *heart pounded*, *hand shook* – show fear instead of saying *he was scared*.","**A question:** *Who – or what – was inside?* – the reader wonders too.","**Slowing down time:** *He turned it slowly… slowly…* – delays the answer.","**Delayed reveal + twist:** *two yellow eyes* first, then the harmless kitten – tension, then relief."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Abdullah's scene. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ مشهد عبدالله واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Why was Khalid still at school?","answer":"He was looking for the football he had forgotten.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"كان يبحث عن الكرة.","options":["He was looking for the football he had forgotten.","He was in a detention.","He was waiting for his father.","He was cleaning the storeroom."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What sound did Khalid hear from the storeroom?","answer":"Scratch. Scratch. Scratch.","explanation":"Sound words.","explanation_ar":"خربشة.","options":["Bang. Bang. Bang.","Knock. Knock. Knock.","Scratch. Scratch. Scratch.","Buzz. Buzz. Buzz."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Khalid see first when the door opened?","answer":"two yellow eyes in the darkness","explanation":"Delayed reveal.","explanation_ar":"عينين صفراوين.","options":["a gray kitten","two yellow eyes in the darkness","a roll of tape","his football"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What was really making the noise?","answer":"a thin gray kitten with its paw caught in tape","explanation":"Twist.","explanation_ar":"قطة صغيرة.","options":["a thin gray kitten with its paw caught in tape","a thief","a ghost","the wind"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which sentence from the storeroom scene is a body reaction that shows fear?","answer":"Khalid's heart pounded.","explanation":"Body reaction.","explanation_ar":"قلبه يخفق.","options":["The school was silent.","The door creaked open.","Everyone had gone home.","Khalid's heart pounded."],"dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How did Khalid feel at the end?","answer":"relieved and amused","explanation":"Inference: he laughed.","explanation_ar":"مرتاح ومستمتع.","options":["terrified","angry","bored","relieved and amused"],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Suspense techniques. Choose.","instructions_ar":"أساليب التشويق: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Why does Abdullah write *He froze. The sound stopped.* as two short sentences?","answer":"Short sentences make the moment feel tense and fast.","explanation":"Short sentences.","explanation_ar":"لتزيد التوتر.","options":["He forgot how to write long sentences.","To make the story longer.","To describe the setting.","Short sentences make the moment feel tense and fast."],"dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What is the effect of *He turned it slowly… slowly…*?","answer":"It slows down time and delays the reveal.","explanation":"Slow motion.","explanation_ar":"يبطئ الزمن ويؤخر الكشف.","options":["It shows Khalid is lazy.","It slows down time and delays the reveal.","It speeds up the story.","It describes the weather."],"dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word in the storeroom scene is an example of onomatopoeia?","answer":"creaked","explanation":"Sound word.","explanation_ar":"creaked.","options":["silent","corridor","creaked","yellow"],"dok":2,"skill":"onomatopoeia","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence is the MOST suspenseful way to describe a noise in an empty library?","answer":"Thud. Somewhere between the dark shelves, a book hit the floor.","explanation":"Suspense makeover.","explanation_ar":"الأكثر تشويقًا.","options":["There was a noise in the library.","Thud. Somewhere between the dark shelves, a book hit the floor.","A book fell.","The library was nice."],"dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which ending is a CLIFFHANGER for a story about a dark museum?","answer":"Then, behind the glass case, something moved.","explanation":"Cliffhanger.","explanation_ar":"نهاية معلقة.","options":["Then, behind the glass case, something moved.","It was only the guard, and everyone went home happy.","The museum closed at five.","The end."],"dok":3,"skill":"endings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the storeroom door keeps the past tense correctly?","answer":"The door creaked, and Khalid froze.","explanation":"Past simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The door creaks, and Khalid froze.","The door creaked, and Khalid freezes.","The door creaked, and Khalid froze.","The door creak, and Khalid freeze."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Make it suspenseful. Use the word bank.","instructions_ar":"اجعلها مشوّقة: استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"________, tap, tap. Someone was knocking on the window.","answer":"Tap","explanation":"Onomatopoeia.","explanation_ar":"طرق.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Her heart ________ as the footsteps came closer.","answer":"pounded","explanation":"Body reaction.","explanation_ar":"خفق بشدة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"He ________, unable to move a single muscle.","answer":"froze","explanation":"Body reaction.","explanation_ar":"تجمّد.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The old gate ________ open in the wind.","answer":"creaked","explanation":"Sound word.","explanation_ar":"صرّ.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Who – or ________ – was hiding behind the curtain?","answer":"what","explanation":"Question.","explanation_ar":"ماذا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"She lifted the lid slowly… ________…","answer":"slowly","explanation":"Slow motion.","explanation_ar":"ببطء.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Build suspense.","instructions_ar":"ابنِ التشويق.","items":[{"q":"Rewrite this flat sentence to build suspense: *Sara found a note in her desk.*","answer":"Sara opened her desk. Something white was hiding under her books. Slowly, she pulled it out. A note – and it had her name on it.","explanation":"Makeover.","explanation_ar":"إعادة كتابة.","dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write two body reactions that show fear without using the word *scared*.","answer":"His mouth went dry. Her knees began to shake.","explanation":"Show, don't tell.","explanation_ar":"أظهر لا تقل.","dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a different ending for Abdullah's scene that is a cliffhanger.","answer":"The door creaked open. In the darkness, two yellow eyes stared back at him. Then, from deeper inside, something else began to scratch.","explanation":"Cliffhanger.","explanation_ar":"نهاية معلقة.","dok":3,"skill":"endings","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a 4–6 sentence suspense scene: *A strange sound at night in your house.* Delay the reveal.","answer":"It was midnight. Everyone was asleep. Then I heard it – drip… drip… drip. I crept down the dark hallway, my heart pounding. The sound was coming from the kitchen. I switched on the light. A leaking tap!","explanation":"Write.","explanation_ar":"اكتب.","dok":3,"skill":"writing suspense","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Suspense Makeover Challenge","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Suspense meter: the child chooses techniques to rewrite a flat sentence; each technique raises a suspense meter. When the meter is full, the scene plays with dramatic music and a twist or cliffhanger.","Each team draws a flat sentence and three technique cards.","In three minutes, they rewrite it using all three techniques.","They read it aloud dramatically.","Other teams name the techniques and raise the suspense meter with a score from 1 to 5.","Each correct technique = 1 point; average audience score = bonus points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"In a mystery story, what is *suspense*?","answer":"the nervous, excited feeling of waiting to find out what will happen","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"التشويق.","options":["a funny ending","the title of a story","a list of characters","the nervous, excited feeling of waiting to find out what will happen"],"dok":1,"skill":"suspense"},{"q":"What is a *cliffhanger*?","answer":"an exciting ending that leaves the reader wondering what happens next","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"نهاية معلقة.","options":["an exciting ending that leaves the reader wondering what happens next","a story about mountains","the first sentence","a happy ending"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*His laugh **echoed** down the empty corridor.* *Echoed* means ___.","answer":"was heard again as it bounced off the walls","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"تردد صداه.","options":["stopped suddenly","was heard again as it bounced off the walls","became very quiet","got lost"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which word is onomatopoeia?","answer":"thud","explanation":"Sound word.","explanation_ar":"thud.","options":["thud","dark","slowly","corridor"],"dok":1,"skill":"onomatopoeia"},{"q":"Which sentence about the power cut keeps the past tense correctly?","answer":"The lights flickered, and everyone gasped.","explanation":"Past simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The lights flicker, and everyone gasped.","The lights flickered, and everyone gasps.","The lights flickered, and everyone gasped.","The lights flickering, and everyone gasp."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense"},{"q":"Why do writers use SHORT sentences at scary moments?","answer":"They make the reader read faster and feel tense.","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"تزيد التوتر.","options":["They make the reader read faster and feel tense.","They are easier to spell.","They make the story calmer.","They describe the weather."],"dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques"},{"q":"Which is a BODY REACTION that shows fear?","answer":"Her hands began to tremble.","explanation":"Show, don't tell.","explanation_ar":"ارتعاش اليدين.","options":["She was very scared.","Her hands began to tremble.","She liked the dark.","She opened the book."],"dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques"},{"q":"Why does Abdullah show *two yellow eyes* before the kitten?","answer":"to delay the reveal and keep the reader guessing","explanation":"Delayed reveal.","explanation_ar":"لتأخير الكشف.","options":["because kittens have no bodies","to describe the weather","to make the story shorter","to delay the reveal and keep the reader guessing"],"dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques"},{"q":"Which opening builds the MOST suspense?","answer":"The house was dark, and the only sound was the slow tick of a clock.","explanation":"Setting.","explanation_ar":"الأكثر تشويقًا.","options":["My house is big.","The house was dark, and the only sound was the slow tick of a clock.","I like my house.","My house has four rooms."],"dok":3,"skill":"suspense techniques"},{"q":"How is a twist DIFFERENT from a cliffhanger?","answer":"A twist is a surprise that answers the question; a cliffhanger stops before the answer.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"المفاجأة تجيب، والمعلّقة تتوقف قبل الإجابة.","options":["They are the same thing.","A twist always happens at the start.","A twist is a surprise that answers the question; a cliffhanger stops before the answer.","A cliffhanger is always funny."],"dok":3,"skill":"endings"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'suspense': a reader holding a book tightly, eyes wide"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'freeze': a boy standing very still in a corridor"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'creak': an old wooden door opening"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'echo': sound waves in an empty hallway"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'cliffhanger': a character hanging from a cliff edge"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'twist': a twisted road sign"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'onomatopoeia': comic-style sound words: BANG! CREAK!"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-01","prompt":"A boy opens a storeroom door to find a kitten."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-02","prompt":"A toolkit of suspense techniques."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS4-03","prompt":"A suspense meter."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V01","script":"suspense. The story was full of suspense."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V02","script":"freeze. He froze when he heard the noise."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V03","script":"creak. The door creaked open."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V04","script":"echo. His laugh echoed down the corridor."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V05","script":"cliffhanger. The chapter ended with a cliffhanger."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V06","script":"twist. The kitten was a funny twist."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-V07","script":"onomatopoeia. *Scratch* and *creak* are onomatopoeia."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-T1","script":"The school was silent. Everyone had gone home, and only Khalid was left, looking for the football he had forgotten. As he passed the old storeroom at the end of the corridor, he heard it. *Scratch. Scratch. Scratch.* He froze. The sound stopped. Then it came again – closer this time, right behind the door. Khalid's heart pounded. His hand shook as he reached for the handle. Who – or what – was inside? The metal was cold under his fingers. He turned it slowly… slowly… The door creaked open. In the darkness, two yellow eyes stared back at him. \"Meow!\" A thin gray kitten blinked up at him, its paw caught in a roll of tape. Khalid laughed so loudly that the sound echoed down the empty corridor."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS4-VID","script":"Why can't you stop reading a good mystery? It's called suspense – and you can learn to build it! The school was silent… Scratch. Scratch. Scratch. He froze… Short sentences. Sound words. A pounding heart. A question. Slow motion… Two yellow eyes… and then – meow! A twist! Or stop at the scariest moment – a cliffhanger! Then the lights went out…"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A three-paragraph mystery story","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a three-paragraph mystery story with the Mystery Story Planner","write 150–200 words with a problem, suspects, fair clues, a red herring, suspense and a logical solution","revise, edit and share the story in a class 'Mystery Night' reading"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لقصة غموض من ثلاث فقرات بمخطط قصة الغموض.","كتابة 150–200 كلمة فيها مشكلة ومشتبه بهم وقرائن عادلة ودليل مضلل وتشويق وحل منطقي.","تحسين القصة وتدقيقها ومشاركتها في أمسية «ليلة الغموض» الصفية."],"success_criteria":["Paragraph 1 introduces the setting and the problem with a suspenseful hook.","Paragraph 2 includes suspects, at least three clues, a red herring and suspense techniques.","Paragraph 3 reveals a logical solution that matches the clues (150–200 words in total)."],"success_criteria_ar":["الفقرة الأولى تقدّم المكان والمشكلة بجملة جذب مشوّقة.","الفقرة الثانية فيها مشتبه بهم وثلاث قرائن على الأقل ودليل مضلل وأساليب تشويق.","الفقرة الثالثة تكشف حلًا منطقيًا يتوافق مع القرائن (150–200 كلمة إجمالًا)."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"vanish","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈvænɪʃ/","definition":"to disappear suddenly","arabic":"يختفي فجأة","example":"The sandwich vanished from the bag.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"crumb","pos":"noun","ipa":"/krʌm/","definition":"a very small piece of bread or cake","arabic":"فتات","example":"There were crumbs on the windowsill.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"windowsill","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈwɪndoʊˌsɪl/","definition":"the flat shelf at the bottom of a window","arabic":"حافة النافذة","example":"A feather lay on the windowsill.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"latch","pos":"noun","ipa":"/lætʃ/","definition":"a small metal bar that keeps a window or a door closed","arabic":"مزلاج","example":"A white feather was caught on the latch.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"judge","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dʒʌdʒ/","definition":"to form an opinion about someone or something","arabic":"يحكم على","example":"Never judge without evidence.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"engrave","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪnˈɡreɪv/","definition":"to cut words or pictures into metal, glass or stone","arabic":"ينقش / يحفر","example":"The shop engraved the names on the trophy.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"solution","pos":"noun","ipa":"/səˈluʃən/","definition":"the answer to a problem or a mystery","arabic":"الحل","example":"The solution surprised everyone.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **mystery story** gives readers a **puzzle** to solve. At this level, write it in **three paragraphs**: **Paragraph 1 – the problem:** set the **scene** (where? when? who?) and show the **problem** with a **suspenseful hook**. **Paragraph 2 – the investigation:** the detective meets the **suspects**, finds at least **three clues** and is fooled for a moment by a **red herring**; build **suspense** before the reveal. **Paragraph 3 – the solution:** **reveal** what really happened, show **how the clues fit**, and end with a **feeling, a lesson or a twist**.\n\nPlay **fair**: every clue must appear **before** the solution, and the solution must be **logical** – no magic and no new information at the end.\n\nUse the **past simple** (*examined, noticed, found*), **time signals** (*The next morning, Suddenly, Then*), **context-rich vocabulary** and **suspense techniques** (short sentences, sound words, body reactions, slow motion). Keep it **150–200 words**.","examples":["Hook + problem: ***For the third day in a row, Tariq opened his bag at lunchtime and froze. His sandwich was gone.***","Vivid detail: ***Only the empty foil remained, crumpled at the bottom like a silver clue.***","Red herring: ***Everyone looked at Bader. He was always hungry, and today he was eating a cheese sandwich exactly like Tariq's.***","Detective's choice: ***But I didn't assume anything. Instead, I examined the classroom.***","Clues: ***Breadcrumbs lay on the windowsill … the window had been left open … a single white feather caught on the latch.***","Suspense: ***I hid behind the curtain and waited… and waited… Suddenly – flap, flap, flap!***","Reveal: ***A huge seagull landed on the windowsill, hopped onto Tariq's desk and dug its beak into his open bag.***","Clues fit: crumbs + open window + white feather → a seagull.","Ending with a lesson: ***From that day on, our class had a new rule: close the window, and never judge without evidence.***"],"common_errors":["✗ A solution with no clues: It was a ghost. → ✓ Plant clues earlier that point to the real answer. (Play fair.)","✗ Revealing the answer in Paragraph 1. → ✓ Keep the secret until Paragraph 3. (Delay the reveal.)","✗ No suspects or only one. → ✓ Give two or three suspects. (Readers need choices.)","✗ A red herring that is never explained. → ✓ Show at the end why it was a false clue. (Close every door.)","✗ I examine the room and found a feather. → ✓ I examined the room and found a feather. (Keep the past tense.)","✗ Too many characters and events. → ✓ One problem, a few suspects, three clues. (Keep it focused.)","✗ Writing 300 words. → ✓ Keep it 150–200 words. (Check the length.)"],"arabic":"قصة الغموض تقدّم للقارئ لغزًا يحله، وتُكتب هنا في ثلاث فقرات: الفقرة الأولى للمشكلة (المكان والزمن والشخصيات مع جملة جذب مشوّقة)، والثانية للتحقيق (المشتبه بهم وثلاث قرائن على الأقل ودليل مضلل وتشويق)، والثالثة للحل (كشف ما حدث وكيف تتوافق القرائن ثم نهاية بشعور أو درس أو مفاجأة).\n\nكن عادلًا: يجب أن تظهر كل القرائن قبل الحل، وأن يكون الحل منطقيًا دون سحر أو معلومات جديدة في النهاية.\n\nاستخدم الماضي البسيط ومؤشرات الزمن وأساليب التشويق، والتزم بطول 150–200 كلمة.\n\nانتبه: اشرح في النهاية لماذا كان الدليل المضلل خاطئًا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"A vanishing lunch… midnight music… a missing trophy. Which mystery will you write?","on_screen":"My Mystery Story 🕵️","visual":"Three mystery cards flip over under a spotlight."},{"time":"00:30–01:20","narration":"Every card has a setting, a problem, suspects, clues, a red herring – and a secret solution.","on_screen":"Mystery Starter Cards","visual":"Icons for each element slide onto a card."},{"time":"01:20–02:40","narration":"For the third day in a row, Tariq opened his bag at lunchtime and froze…","on_screen":"(model story)","visual":"A classroom by the sea; crumbs, an open window, a white feather; a hidden detective; a seagull."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Paragraph 1: the problem. Paragraph 2: the investigation. Paragraph 3: the solution – and play fair!","on_screen":"P1 Problem · P2 Investigation · P3 Solution","visual":"Three case-file folders open one after another."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Read your story at Mystery Night – and see if your classmates can solve it before the end!","on_screen":"Mystery Night 🌙","visual":"Students read with flashlights in a dim classroom."}],"captions_ar":["غداء يختفي… موسيقى منتصف الليل… كأس مفقودة. أي لغز ستكتب؟","في كل بطاقة مكان ومشكلة ومشتبه بهم وقرائن ودليل مضلل وحل سري.","لليوم الثالث على التوالي فتح طارق حقيبته وقت الغداء وتجمّد في مكانه…","الفقرة الأولى: المشكلة. الثانية: التحقيق. الثالثة: الحل، وكن عادلًا!","اقرأ قصتك في ليلة الغموض، وانظر هل يحل زملاؤك اللغز قبل النهاية!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, mysterious female voice that turns playful at the reveal, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Mystery Starter Cards","body":"**Card A – The Vanishing Lunch** · Setting: a Grade 6 classroom near the Jeddah Corniche · Problem: for three days, Tariq's sandwich has disappeared from his bag at break · Suspects: Bader (always hungry), the cleaner, a new student · Clues: breadcrumbs on the windowsill, the window left open at break, a white feather on the latch · Red herring: Bader is eating a sandwich exactly like Tariq's · Solution: a clever seagull flies in through the open window\n\n**Card B – The Midnight Music** · Setting: Grandfather's old house in Taif during the holidays · Problem: every night, someone plays a few notes on Grandfather's oud · Suspects: cousin Majid (loves music), the neighbor's son · Clues: the oud lies near the open balcony door, tiny paw prints in the dust, the notes always come after the cat goes out · Red herring: Majid hums the same song in the morning · Solution: the family cat walks across the oud strings to reach the balcony\n\n**Card C – The Missing Trophy** · Setting: the school office the day before the awards ceremony · Problem: the class science trophy is missing from its shelf · Suspects: Yazan (wanted to hold it), the PE teacher, a visitor · Clues: a receipt from an engraving shop on the desk, the shelf is not dusty, the principal's car is gone · Red herring: Yazan has a photo of himself with the trophy · Solution: the principal took it to have the winners' names engraved\n\n**Card D – Your Own Mystery** · Choose a setting, a problem, two or three suspects, three clues, a red herring and a logical solution.","word_count":254,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U3-LS5-1","genre":"story planning cards","role":"model","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **one card**. You (or a character) will be the **detective**.","Plan **three paragraphs**: (1) setting + problem with a suspenseful hook; (2) investigation – suspects, clues, a red herring and suspense; (3) the solution revealed with reasoning and an ending.","Play **fair**: put every clue in Paragraph 2 before you reveal the solution in Paragraph 3."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the cards and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ البطاقات والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where is the classroom in *The Vanishing Lunch*?","answer":"near the Jeddah Corniche","explanation":"Card A.","explanation_ar":"قرب كورنيش جدة.","options":["in Taif","near the Jeddah Corniche","in the school office","in Riyadh"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which clue on Card B points to the cat?","answer":"tiny paw prints in the dust","explanation":"Card B.","explanation_ar":"آثار مخالب صغيرة.","options":["Majid hums the same song","the oud is old","the house is in Taif","tiny paw prints in the dust"],"dok":1,"skill":"clues","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Who really took the trophy on Card C?","answer":"the principal, to have names engraved","explanation":"Card C.","explanation_ar":"المدير لنقش الأسماء.","options":["Yazan","the PE teacher","the principal, to have names engraved","a visitor"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did the narrator find caught on the window latch in the model?","answer":"a single white feather","explanation":"Clue.","explanation_ar":"ريشة بيضاء.","options":["a piece of foil","a cheese sandwich","a key","a single white feather"],"dok":1,"skill":"clues","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why did everyone suspect Bader at first?","answer":"He was always hungry and had a sandwich exactly like Tariq's.","explanation":"Red herring.","explanation_ar":"لأنه جائع وشطيرته مثل شطيرة طارق.","options":["He was always hungry and had a sandwich exactly like Tariq's.","He sat by the window.","He had a feather.","He arrived late."],"dok":2,"skill":"red herrings","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How does the model explain the red herring at the end?","answer":"Bader's mother simply made the same sandwiches as Tariq's mother.","explanation":"Closing the red herring.","explanation_ar":"والدته تصنع الشطائر نفسها.","options":["Bader stole the sandwich after all.","Bader fed the seagull.","Bader's mother simply made the same sandwiches as Tariq's mother.","It is never explained."],"dok":2,"skill":"red herrings","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan your mystery. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطّط لقصة الغموض: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST suspenseful hook for *The Midnight Music*?","answer":"At exactly midnight, a single note floated through Grandfather's dark house – and everyone was asleep.","explanation":"Hook.","explanation_ar":"أفضل جملة جذب.","options":["Grandfather has an oud.","This story is about music.","Taif is a city.","At exactly midnight, a single note floated through Grandfather's dark house – and everyone was asleep."],"dok":2,"skill":"hooks","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which detail should you put in Paragraph 2 of *The Missing Trophy*, BEFORE the solution?","answer":"a receipt from an engraving shop on the desk","explanation":"Play fair.","explanation_ar":"قرينة قبل الحل.","options":["a receipt from an engraving shop on the desk","The principal smiled and showed the engraved names.","Everyone cheered at the ceremony.","The trophy was back on the shelf."],"dok":2,"skill":"clues","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which solution for *The Midnight Music* is LOGICAL and fair?","answer":"The cat walked across the oud strings on its way to the balcony.","explanation":"Logical solution.","explanation_ar":"حل منطقي.","options":["The cat walked across the oud strings on its way to the balcony.","A ghost played the oud.","The oud played by itself with magic.","A stranger nobody had mentioned climbed in."],"dok":2,"skill":"solutions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence from a mystery draft keeps the past tense correctly?","answer":"I examined the shelf and noticed it was not dusty.","explanation":"Past simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["I examine the shelf and noticed it was not dusty.","I examined the shelf and notice it is not dusty.","I examined the shelf and noticed it was not dusty.","I examining the shelf and noticed it was not dusty."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence from a trophy mystery builds the MOST suspense?","answer":"Slowly, I opened the office door. The shelf was empty.","explanation":"Suspense.","explanation_ar":"الأكثر تشويقًا.","options":["I opened the door, and the trophy was gone, and I was sad.","The trophy was gold.","The office has a shelf.","Slowly, I opened the office door. The shelf was empty."],"dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A partner's mystery ends with a new character nobody has met before. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Introduce that character or the clues about them earlier in Paragraph 2.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"قدّمه في الفقرة الثانية.","options":["Add more new characters at the end.","Introduce that character or the clues about them earlier in Paragraph 2.","Remove all the clues.","Reveal the solution in the first sentence."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan your story with the Mystery Story Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط لقصتك بمخطط قصة الغموض.","items":[{"q":"Setting, characters and the problem with a hook.","answer":"Grandfather's old house in Taif, winter holiday; me, cousin Majid, Grandfather. Hook: At exactly midnight, a single note floated through the dark house.","explanation":"P1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة 1.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Suspects and red herring.","answer":"Majid (loves music) and the neighbor's son; red herring: Majid hums the same song in the morning.","explanation":"Suspects.","explanation_ar":"المشتبه بهم.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Three clues in order and a suspense moment.","answer":"oud near the open balcony door → tiny paw prints in the dust → notes always come after the cat goes out; I hide behind the sofa in the dark and wait…","explanation":"Clues + suspense.","explanation_ar":"القرائن والتشويق.","dok":3,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Solution, how the clues fit and the ending.","answer":"The cat walks across the strings to reach the balcony; the paw prints and timing fit; Majid hums the song because he heard it at night; ending: Grandfather names the cat 'the Musician'.","explanation":"Solution.","explanation_ar":"الحل.","dok":3,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and share your mystery.","instructions_ar":"اكتب قصة الغموض وحسّنها ودققها وشاركها.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1: setting and problem with a suspenseful hook.","answer":"At exactly midnight, a single note floated through Grandfather's dark house in Taif. Then another. Everyone was supposed to be asleep. The next morning, Grandfather's old oud lay on the floor near the balcony door. \"Who has been playing my oud?\" he asked.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الأولى.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 2: suspects, clues, a red herring and suspense.","answer":"Everyone looked at my cousin Majid, who was humming the same tune at breakfast. But I didn't assume anything. I examined the oud and found tiny paw prints in the dust around it. I also noticed that the notes always began after our cat went out. That night, I hid behind the sofa and waited… and waited…","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثانية.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 3: the solution and the ending.","answer":"Plink! Our gray cat strolled across the strings on her way to the balcony. Majid had only been humming the tune he heard at night. Grandfather laughed and named the cat \"the Musician.\"","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثالثة.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence you improved to make it more suspenseful (before → after).","answer":"Before: I saw the cat play the oud. After: Plink! A gray shadow strolled across the strings.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Mystery Story Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Choose a card. Plan each part, and make sure every clue appears before the solution.","items":[{"q":"Setting and characters","answer":"Grade 6 classroom near the Jeddah Corniche; Tariq; me (the detective); Bader"},{"q":"Problem + suspenseful hook","answer":"For the third day, Tariq's sandwich has vanished – only crumpled foil left."},{"q":"Suspects and red herring","answer":"Bader (always hungry), the cleaner – red herring: Bader's sandwich looks exactly like Tariq's."},{"q":"Three clues (in the order the detective finds them)","answer":"breadcrumbs on the windowsill → window open at break → white feather on the latch"},{"q":"Suspense moment before the reveal","answer":"I hide behind the curtain… wait… flap, flap, flap!"},{"q":"Solution + how the clues fit + ending","answer":"A seagull steals the sandwich; crumbs + open window + feather fit; Bader gets an apology; lesson: never judge without evidence."}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U3-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Choose a Mystery Starter Card (or create your own with Card D). Write a three-paragraph mystery story (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: the setting and the problem with a suspenseful hook. Paragraph 2: the investigation with suspects, at least three clues, a red herring and a suspense moment. Paragraph 3: the solution, how the clues fit and an ending with a feeling, a lesson or a twist. Choose a Mystery Starter Card (or create your own with Card D). Write a three-paragraph mystery story (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: the setting and the problem with a suspenseful hook. Paragraph 2: the investigation with suspects, at least three clues, a red herring and a suspense moment. Paragraph 3: the solution, how the clues fit and an ending with a feeling, a lesson or a twist.","instructions_ar":"اختر بطاقة من بطاقات بداية الغموض (أو أنشئ لغزك ببطاقة D)، واكتب قصة غموض من ثلاث فقرات (150–200 كلمة). الفقرة الأولى: المكان والمشكلة بجملة جذب مشوّقة. الثانية: التحقيق مع المشتبه بهم وثلاث قرائن على الأقل ودليل مضلل ولحظة تشويق. الثالثة: الحل وكيف تتوافق القرائن ونهاية بشعور أو درس أو مفاجأة.","prompt":"Choose a Mystery Starter Card (or create your own with Card D). Write a three-paragraph mystery story (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: the setting and the problem with a suspenseful hook. Paragraph 2: the investigation with suspects, at least three clues, a red herring and a suspense moment. Paragraph 3: the solution, how the clues fit and an ending with a feeling, a lesson or a twist.","checklist":["My hook is suspenseful and shows the problem.","I described the setting and the characters briefly.","I included two or three suspects and a red herring.","I planted at least three clues before the solution.","I used at least three suspense techniques.","My solution is logical and explains the red herring.","I used the past simple and time signals correctly.","My story has three paragraphs and 150–200 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":150,"max_words":220,"organizer":"Mystery Story Planner (setting – problem – suspects and red herring – clues – suspense – solution)","items":[{"q":"Choose a Mystery Starter Card (or create your own with Card D). Write a three-paragraph mystery story (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: the setting and the problem with a suspenseful hook. Paragraph 2: the investigation with suspects, at least three clues, a red herring and a suspense moment. Paragraph 3: the solution, how the clues fit and an ending with a feeling, a lesson or a twist.","answer":"For the third day in a row, Tariq opened his bag at lunchtime and froze. His sandwich was gone. Only the empty foil remained, crumpled at the bottom like a silver clue. \"Someone in this class is a thief,\" he whispered. (new paragraph) Everyone looked at Bader. He was always hungry, and today he was eating a cheese sandwich exactly like Tariq's. But I didn't assume anything. Instead, I examined the classroom. Breadcrumbs lay on the windowsill, and the window had been left open during break. Then I spotted it: a single white feather caught on the latch. The next morning, I hid behind the curtain and waited… and waited… Suddenly – flap, flap, flap! (new paragraph) A huge seagull landed on the windowsill, hopped onto Tariq's desk and dug its beak into his open bag. The whole class burst out laughing, and Bader finally got his apology. His mother, it turned out, simply made the same sandwiches as Tariq's mother. From that day on, our class had a new rule: close the window, and never judge without evidence.","_w":{"checklist":["My hook is suspenseful and shows the problem.","I described the setting and the characters briefly.","I included two or three suspects and a red herring.","I planted at least three clues before the solution.","I used at least three suspense techniques.","My solution is logical and explains the red herring.","I used the past simple and time signals correctly.","My story has three paragraphs and 150–200 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"min":150,"max":220}}]}],"game":{"name":"Mystery Night: Solve It First!","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Mystery reader: the app reads a story aloud (or the child reads), pauses before Paragraph 3 and asks the listener to choose the solution and the clue that proves it. Correct guesses earn detective stars.","A reader reads Paragraphs 1 and 2 only.","Listeners write their solution and one clue on a slip.","The reader reads Paragraph 3.","Correct solution = 2 points; correct supporting clue = 1 point.","If nobody can solve it, the writer must check that the clues are fair!"],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"How many paragraphs does your mystery story have?","answer":"three","explanation":"Organization.","explanation_ar":"ثلاث.","options":["three","one","two","five"],"dok":1,"skill":"organization"},{"q":"What does *vanish* mean?","answer":"to disappear suddenly","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"يختفي فجأة.","options":["to appear slowly","to break","to share","to disappear suddenly"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*A white feather was caught on the **latch**.* A *latch* is ___.","answer":"a small bar that keeps a window or door closed","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"مزلاج.","options":["a kind of bird","a sandwich wrapper","a school bag","a small bar that keeps a window or door closed"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which part of a mystery story reveals what really happened?","answer":"the solution in Paragraph 3","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"الحل في الفقرة الثالثة.","options":["the hook","the solution in Paragraph 3","the list of suspects","the red herring"],"dok":1,"skill":"mystery elements"},{"q":"Which sentence about the seagull keeps the past tense correctly?","answer":"The seagull landed and grabbed the sandwich.","explanation":"Past simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The seagull lands and grabbed the sandwich.","The seagull landed and grabbed the sandwich.","The seagull landed and grabs the sandwich.","The seagull landing and grabbed the sandwich."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense"},{"q":"What does it mean to *play fair* in a mystery?","answer":"All the clues appear before the solution.","explanation":"Fairness.","explanation_ar":"كل القرائن قبل الحل.","options":["The detective always wins.","The thief is introduced at the end.","All the clues appear before the solution.","There are no suspects."],"dok":2,"skill":"mystery elements"},{"q":"Why should the red herring be explained at the end?","answer":"so readers understand why the false clue misled them","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"ليفهم القارئ سبب التضليل.","options":["so the story is longer","so readers understand why the false clue misled them","so readers forget it","so there are more suspects"],"dok":2,"skill":"red herrings"},{"q":"Which detail would be a GOOD clue for a mystery about a missing school cat?","answer":"small muddy paw prints leading to the storeroom","explanation":"Relevant clue.","explanation_ar":"آثار مخالب.","options":["the school has a red door","it was Tuesday","small muddy paw prints leading to the storeroom","the teacher likes tea"],"dok":2,"skill":"clues"},{"q":"A partner's story says *Suddenly, magic made the trophy return.* What is the problem?","answer":"The solution is not logical and does not match the clues.","explanation":"Logic.","explanation_ar":"الحل غير منطقي.","options":["The solution is not logical and does not match the clues.","It is too short.","It uses the past tense.","It has a hook."],"dok":3,"skill":"solutions"},{"q":"Which ending gives a mystery a strong lesson?","answer":"From that day on, we never judged anyone without evidence.","explanation":"Ending.","explanation_ar":"نهاية بدرس.","options":["Then we went home.","The end of the mystery.","From that day on, we never judged anyone without evidence.","It was a Tuesday."],"dok":3,"skill":"endings"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'vanish': an empty lunch bag with dotted outlines"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'crumb': breadcrumbs on a window ledge"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'windowsill': a windowsill with a feather"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'latch': a window latch close-up"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'judge': a person pointing at a suspect with a question mark"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'engrave': a tool cutting letters into a metal plate"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'solution': a key opening a lock with a light bulb"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-01","prompt":"Students reading mysteries by flashlight."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-02","prompt":"Three mystery starter cards."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U3-LS5-03","prompt":"A magnifying-glass story planner."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V01","script":"vanish. The sandwich vanished from the bag."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V02","script":"crumb. There were crumbs on the windowsill."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V03","script":"windowsill. A feather lay on the windowsill."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V04","script":"latch. A white feather was caught on the latch."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V05","script":"judge. Never judge without evidence."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V06","script":"engrave. The shop engraved the names on the trophy."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-V07","script":"solution. The solution surprised everyone."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-T1","script":"**Card A – The Vanishing Lunch** · Setting: a Grade 6 classroom near the Jeddah Corniche · Problem: for three days, Tariq's sandwich has disappeared from his bag at break · Suspects: Bader (always hungry), the cleaner, a new student · Clues: breadcrumbs on the windowsill, the window left open at break, a white feather on the latch · Red herring: Bader is eating a sandwich exactly like Tariq's · Solution: a clever seagull flies in through the open window **Card B – The Midnight Music** · Setting: Grandfather's old house in Taif during the holidays · Problem: every night, someone plays a few notes on Grandfather's oud · Suspects: cousin Majid (loves music), the neighbor's son · Clues: the oud lies near the open balcony door, tiny paw prints in the dust, the notes always come after the cat goes out · Red herring: Majid hums the same song in the morning · Solution: the family cat walks across the oud strings to reach the balcony **Card C – The Missing Trophy** · Setting: the school office the day before the awards ceremony · Problem: the class science trophy is missing from its shelf · Suspects: Yazan (wanted to hold it), the PE teacher, a visitor · Clues: a receipt from an engraving shop on the desk, the shelf is not dusty, the principal's car is gone · Red herring: Yazan has a photo of himself with the trophy · Solution: the principal took it to have the winners' names engraved **Card D – Your Own Mystery** · Choose a setting, a problem, two or three suspects, three clues, a red herring and a logical solution."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U3-LS5-VID","script":"A vanishing lunch… midnight music… a missing trophy. Which mystery will you write? Every card has a setting, a problem, suspects, clues, a red herring – and a secret solution. For the third day in a row, Tariq opened his bag at lunchtime and froze… Paragraph 1: the problem. Paragraph 2: the investigation. Paragraph 3: the solution – and play fair! Read your story at Mystery Night – and see if your classmates can solve it before the end!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"The police asked every ___ what they had seen on the night of the robbery.","answer":"witness","explanation":"Witness.","explanation_ar":"شاهد.","options":["riddle","footprint","clue","witness"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The doctor will ___ your throat with a small light.","answer":"examine","explanation":"Examine.","explanation_ar":"يفحص.","options":["examine","reveal","vanish","assume"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The ___ dog was found two streets away, sleeping under a car.","answer":"missing","explanation":"Missing.","explanation_ar":"المفقود.","options":["logical","damp","missing","native"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Mona couldn't have broken the vase – she was at school all morning. Her teacher can confirm her ___.","answer":"alibi","explanation":"Alibi.","explanation_ar":"حجة غيابها.","options":["riddle","suspect","mystery","alibi"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Next week, the scientists will ___ their discovery to the public.","answer":"reveal","explanation":"Reveal.","explanation_ar":"يكشفون.","options":["examine","reveal","freeze","creak"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The witness was **reluctant**, or unwilling, to talk to the police.* What does *reluctant* mean?","answer":"unwilling","explanation":"Synonym clue: or.","explanation_ar":"متردد / غير راغب.","options":["excited","lost","unwilling","angry"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Unlike his **reckless** brother, who drives too fast, Sami is very careful.* What does *reckless* mean?","answer":"not careful about danger","explanation":"Contrast clue: unlike.","explanation_ar":"متهور.","options":["very slow","not careful about danger","very careful","very kind"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The footprints were so **faint** that the detective needed a magnifying glass to see them.* What does *faint* mean?","answer":"very hard to see","explanation":"General sense.","explanation_ar":"باهت / خافت.","options":["very hard to see","very large","very muddy","very new"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which word in this sentence is onomatopoeia? *The gate clanged shut behind them.*","answer":"clanged","explanation":"Sound word.","explanation_ar":"clanged.","options":["gate","shut","clanged","behind"],"dok":1,"skill":"onomatopoeia","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence about Rakan keeps the past tense correctly?","answer":"Rakan knelt down and examined the footprints.","explanation":"Past simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Rakan knelt down and examined the footprints.","Rakan kneels down and examined the footprints.","Rakan knelt down and examines the footprints.","Rakan kneeling down and examined the footprints."],"dok":1,"skill":"verb tense","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence builds the MOST suspense?","answer":"Crunch. Something was moving behind the tent.","explanation":"Suspense.","explanation_ar":"الأكثر تشويقًا.","options":["There was a fox near the tent.","Crunch. Something was moving behind the tent.","The tent was green and big.","Rakan liked camping very much."],"dok":2,"skill":"suspense techniques","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"In a fair mystery story, where should all the clues appear?","answer":"before the solution is revealed","explanation":"Play fair.","explanation_ar":"قبل الحل.","options":["only in the last sentence","in the title","after the ending","before the solution is revealed"],"dok":2,"skill":"mystery elements","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"In a mystery plan, which detail is a RED HERRING? (Solution: the wind blew the papers away.)","answer":"A boy was seen holding papers that looked like the missing ones.","explanation":"Red herring.","explanation_ar":"دليل مضلل.","options":["A boy was seen holding papers that looked like the missing ones.","The window was wide open.","Papers were found in the garden.","It was a very windy day."],"dok":2,"skill":"red herrings","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which ending turns *The Thief of the Dunes* into a CLIFFHANGER?","answer":"As Rakan switched off his flashlight, he heard a second, much louder crunch behind him.","explanation":"Cliffhanger.","explanation_ar":"نهاية معلقة.","options":["Uncle Fahd apologized, and everyone laughed.","They packed the tents and drove home.","As Rakan switched off his flashlight, he heard a second, much louder crunch behind him.","The fox was named Big Ears."],"dok":3,"skill":"endings","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Write a three-paragraph mystery story (150–200 words) called *The Midnight Visitor in the Art Room*. Every morning, strange marks appear on the art-room floor. Paragraph 1: the setting and the problem with a suspenseful hook. Paragraph 2: the investigation with suspects, at least three clues, a red herring and a suspense moment. Paragraph 3: a logical solution that matches the clues and an ending.","model":"Every morning that week, the art room looked like a crime scene. Bright blue marks wandered across the white floor, and nobody knew who had made them. Mr. Nasser, our art teacher, was not amused. (new paragraph) Everyone suspected Ziyad, because he had stayed late on Monday to finish his blue painting. But I didn't assume anything. I examined the marks closely. They were not footprints at all – they were thin, wavy lines, as if something had rolled through the paint. All the lines started beside a spilled paint pot and ended at a small charging station in the corner. On Wednesday, with Mr. Nasser's permission, I left a school tablet recording on the top shelf. The next morning, we pressed play and watched the dark, silent room. Then, at exactly midnight, we heard a soft whirr… (new paragraph) The school's new robot vacuum rolled out of its station, bumped into the paint pot and drew a fresh blue line across the floor! Ziyad was cleared, Mr. Nasser laughed, and the paint pots were moved to a high shelf. Our 'midnight visitor' had simply been doing its job.","marks":20}});
